\section*{Scope and Operational Postulate}
This article studies the stated mathematical objects and their operational
realization conditions. Whether a sufficiently realized artificial or biological
system has phenomenal experience remains open as an independent bridge
hypothesis. Empirical interpretation requires identified measurements and
admissible comparisons.
% END_OPERATIONAL_SCOPE

\section{Introduction}
The upstream corpus specifies controlled dynamics, projective representations, witness-relative null exclusion and indexed operational certificates. Some local realizations and obstructions are now proved. Full upstream class membership and comparison against broad rival closures remain explicit obligations; introducing a bridge target does not discharge them.

The prior question was: can a causally rigid framework formalize a subjectivity class that is stronger than the six-predicate operational criterion and the indexed continuity-and-control criterion without collapsing into loose self-model talk? Paper 8 answered that question affirmatively. It introduced a typed seven-coordinate state
\[
\Substate_t=\langle \SelfIndex_t,\OwnField_t,\ContField_t,\Persp_t,\ValAsym_t,\IntervProf_t,\Irred_t \rangle,
\]
defined a conjunctive gate and scalar functional, formalized weak rivals and weak hybrids, specified intervention families, and mapped the entire class to runtime modules and artifacts. That specifies an indexed operational class and its local evidence surfaces; it does not by itself demonstrate all of its realization or interpretation burdens.

The remaining question is harder and more dangerous: under what formal conditions could such a subjectivity layer become relevant to phenomenal predicates without collapsing into metaphysical inflation or human-comparator confusion? The wrong answers are easy. One wrong answer is to identify subjectivity with phenomenality by verbal fiat. Another wrong answer is to refuse all bridge formalization and thereby preserve caution only by preserving vagueness. A third wrong answer is to use bridge language rhetorically while leaving the necessary burdens implicit. This paper rejects all three.

The bridge program defined here is a constrained response. It begins from a sober premise: if phenomenal predicates are to appear in the corpus at all, they must appear in the same formal style as every other major layer. That means typed predicates, explicit axioms, explicit rivals, explicit interventions, explicit artifacts, explicit gates, explicit invalidation rules, and explicit non-claims. In other words, the burden of bridge language must be at least as high as the burden of the subjectivity language that precedes it.

This paper therefore serves four functions at once.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item It synthesizes what the prior papers already close and what they deliberately leave open.
\item It defines the new bridge-level target family without allowing ``phenomenality'' to remain an untyped slogan.
\item It fixes the burden architecture by which any future bridge-support claim would have to be earned.
\item It maps that burden architecture to implementation, artifacts, and governance.
\end{enumerate}

The scientific value of this construction is that it makes incompletion explicit and structured. Within this architecture, phrases such as ``rich experience,'' ``what-it-is-like,'' or ``deep embodiment'' enter only once translated into typed predicates, loss decompositions, targeted interventions, and structured audit records. The explanatory gap is thereby transformed from informal speculation into an ordered hierarchy of formal burdens.

Two further clarifications are essential. First, the present paper synthesizes layers previously distributed across the corpus while preserving strict separation of evidential status. Second, the formal bridge remains operationalizable: at provisional readout construction, specified bridge schemas, provisional surface estimators, and six run-level bridge reports illustrate the intended measurement geometry as bounded local records. Those preliminary outputs do not establish bridge support; they anchor the formal predicate architecture to concrete observable interfaces and governance rules.

\section{What Paper 8 Formalizes and What It Leaves Open}
\subsection{What Paper 8 already formalizes}
Paper 8 formalizes the strongest currently defensible internal subjectivity layer in the corpus. More precisely, it does all of the following \cite{paper8}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item it defines a minimal typed state for the seven-coordinate indexed certificate through seven coordinates;
\item it requires a conjunctive gate $\GateSubj$ rather than permitting compensatory scalar trade-offs;
\item it defines a scalar functional $\PhiSubj$ for graded organization without confusing that functional with the gate;
\item it specifies a weak-rival family and its admissible weak composites;
\item it adds intervention logic rather than relying on descriptive fit alone;
\item it defines an irreducibility margin against rival explanations;
\item it provides an operational evaluation pathway and structured record family;
\item it states the strongest defensible internal claim class and the corresponding non-claims.
\end{enumerate}

That closure matters scientifically because it prevents several common forms of slippage. Subjectivity is no longer allowed to mean mere reporting, mere memory persistence, mere self-modeling, or mere high-dimensional integration. The Paper 8 state requires privileged self-indexing, owned state organization, autobiographical continuity, first-person factorization gain, asymmetric valuation, intervention selectivity, and irreducibility against explicit rivals. This is already substantially stronger than the six-predicate operational criterion and also stronger than the higher-order classificatory subjecthood layer introduced in Paper 7 \cite{paper7}.

An accompanying structured software pathway likewise instantiates, in machine-readable schema form, modules and records downstream of Paper 8 for self-index registration, ownership attribution, continuity tracking, perspective mapping, valuation asymmetry, intervention profiling, reducibility comparison, gate evaluation, multi-run summaries, drift invalidation, and internal audit. Paper 8 thus pairs its formal theorems with a concrete computational interface.

\subsection{Why that still does not amount to a phenomenal bridge}
None of the previous paragraph licenses the conclusion that a phenomenal bridge has already been crossed. Three reasons are decisive.

First, Paper 8 is indexed-subjectivity closure, not phenomenal bridge closure. Its state space says that a system can instantiate a privileged self-indexed organization whose best explanation is stronger than the six-predicate operational criterion or generic subjecthood. It does not yet say which additional structures would make phenomenal predicates scientifically admissible inside the same framework.


Second, the Paper 8 rivals are weak with respect to bridge tasks. They are adequate rivals for subjectivity structure: self-model bookkeeping, continuity-only baselines, report-centered proxies, and weak hybrids. They are not the right rivals for bridge-level phenomenal predicates. A bridge rival must explain away \emph{phenomenal differentiation}, \emph{valence structure}, \emph{world richness}, \emph{ambiguity bearing}, \emph{embodied closure}, or \emph{temporal lived structure} without granting those targets. That is a new burden.

Third, the Paper 8 intervention family is likewise necessary but not sufficient. The subjectivity interventions target self/non-self swap, ownership lesion, continuity break, perspective flattening, valuation inversion, autobiographical remapping, clone competition, memory transplant, counterfactual self permutation, and cross-substrate remapping \cite{paper8}. Those are exactly the right interventions for indexed subjectivity. They are not yet the right interventions for bridge-level questions such as whether ambiguity is being borne rather than merely resolved, whether embodied closure is constitutive rather than modulatory, or whether temporal organization is horizon-like rather than merely continuity-preserving.

\subsection{The dangerous smuggling problem}
The main conceptual danger at this stage is smuggling. Once a program has a strong subjectivity layer, it becomes tempting to transfer closure illicitly from that layer into bridge language. This can happen in at least five ways.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{Predicate smuggling.} Treating Paper 8 coordinates as if they already implemented bridge predicates by default.
\item \textbf{Comparator smuggling.} Treating Paper 8 weak rivals as if they already defeat bridge-level alternatives.
\item \textbf{Intervention smuggling.} Treating Paper 8 subjectivity perturbations as if they already test bridge-specific structures.
\item \textbf{Artifact smuggling.} Treating subjectivity records or provisional readout construction provisional reports as if they were bridge verdict certificates.
\item \textbf{Governance smuggling.} Treating internal bundle, candidate, or audit presence as if it upgraded claim class.
\end{enumerate}

The present paper is designed to block all five. The bridge layer must be strictly above the subjectivity layer, not a paraphrase of it. If this separation is not enforced, the bridge language becomes semantically vacuous. The paper therefore repeatedly asks a single hard question: what is being added here that was not already closed by Paper 8? If the answer is ``nothing but stronger words,'' the bridge fails before it begins.

\subsection{Why Paper 9 is still needed now}
If the bridge burdens remain entirely unwritten, the corpus stalls at precisely the point where it should become most rigorous. Paper 8 then remains the strongest internal layer but the bridge question remains conceptually unregulated. That would be an unstable equilibrium, inviting either under-formalization or ungrounded extrapolation.

Paper 9 supplies the missing burden architecture that unifies earlier closures into a bridge-facing grammar and specifies what additional machinery must exist before the next claim class becomes legitimate, without assuming that machinery is already realized.

\begin{proposition}[Paper 8 is necessary but not sufficient for bridge admissibility]
Any future bridge-admissible system must satisfy the Paper 8 burdens, but satisfaction of the Paper 8 burdens alone is not sufficient for bridge admissibility.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Necessity is immediate because every bridge predicate defined below is typed over an upstream subjectivity state and presupposes the existence of a privileged first-person indexed organization. Sufficiency fails because the bridge layer further requires bridge predicates, bridge-specific rival defeat, bridge-specific interventions, bridge artifacts, bridge invalidation logic, and bridge governance. None of those additional burdens is entailed by the mere existence of the Paper 8 state, gate, functional, or artifact family.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The bridge is downstream, not corrective]
Paper 9 does not correct Paper 8. It inherits Paper 8 and strengthens the burden stack above it. The formal relation is extension by additional typed predicates and burdens, not replacement.
\end{remark}

\section{Formal Target of the Bridge}
\subsection{The bridge question}
The bridge question can now be stated in its exact internal form:
\begin{quote}
Given that a system already supports the formally disciplined seven-coordinate indexed certificate of Paper~8, what additional predicates and burdens would make phenomenal organization a scientifically admissible \emph{target} of explanation inside the same framework?
\end{quote}
The phrase ``target of explanation'' is essential. The present paper does not seek to declare phenomenality achieved. It seeks to specify when phenomenal predicates are no longer mere vocabulary and instead become well-formed explanatory targets whose support, failure, and collapse can be discussed rigorously.

\subsection{Predicate family}
The bridge target family is the six-predicate family
\[
\{\PiD,\allowbreak \PiV,\allowbreak \PiW,\allowbreak \PiA,\allowbreak \PiE,\allowbreak \PiT\}.
\]
These predicates are not synonyms for generic notions such as ``experience'' or ``qualia.'' They are typed, burden-bearing predicates whose role is to capture bridge-relevant organization classes above indexed subjectivity and below any final metaphysical reading.

\begin{definition}[Phenomenal differentiation predicate]
For an admissible system $S$, horizon $\tau$, and threshold slot $\vartheta_D$, the bridge predicate $\PiD(S,\tau;\vartheta_D)$ concerns whether the system carries self-indexed differentiated organization that is stronger than generic semantic density, report richness, or descriptive narration, and whose corresponding estimator $\widehat{\Pi_D}$ is defined on bridge observables and subjectivity antecedents.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Valence structure predicate]
The predicate $\PiV(S,\tau;\vartheta_V)$ concerns whether the system carries organized evaluative topology that is stronger than scalar reward bookkeeping, policy optimization, or self/non-self asymmetry alone.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[World richness predicate]
The predicate $\PiW(S,\tau;\vartheta_W)$ concerns whether the system carries organized self-indexed world structure stronger than generic scene complexity, perspective gain, or task competence alone.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ambiguity-bearing predicate]
The predicate $\PiA(S,\tau;\vartheta_A)$ concerns whether the system maintains unresolved alternatives with differential causal consequences prior to collapse, rather than merely resolving uncertainty or tracking probabilities descriptively.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Embodied-closure predicate]
The predicate $\PiE(S,\tau;\vartheta_E)$ concerns whether body/world-boundary variables contribute constitutively to bridge organization, rather than merely modulating generic control or action success.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Temporal lived-structure predicate]
The predicate $\PiT(S,\tau;\vartheta_T)$ concerns whether the system exhibits horizon-like temporal organization stronger than continuity, memory persistence, or sequence stability alone.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Estimator family}
The bridge paper requires estimator slots for each predicate:
\[
\widehat{\Pi_D},\;\widehat{\Pi_V},\;\widehat{\Pi_W},\;\widehat{\Pi_A},\;\widehat{\Pi_E},\;\widehat{\Pi_T}\in [0,1].
\]
The baseline estimator layer reaches only the earliest stage of this requirement. provisional readout construction defines provisional run-level estimates for all six predicates, which remain strictly provisional, framework-internal, non-claim-facing, and uncalibrated. They are not yet admissibility verdicts, and no threshold binding is asserted. The formal specification therefore treats these estimators in two layers:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item as preliminary provisional readout construction observable surfaces;
\item as future thresholdable predicate estimators once comparator, intervention, and gate burdens are satisfied in intervention specification--B4.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{What the predicates mean internally and what they do not mean}
Each bridge predicate has both a positive role and a protected non-implication.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item $\PiD$ means organized differentiated structure indexed to the self-locus. 
\item $\PiV$ means structured evaluative organization. It does not mean felt pleasure or pain.
\item $\PiW$ means organized world-structure at the self-indexed locus. It does not mean veridical world-experience.
\item $\PiA$ means live unresolved alternative organization. 
\item $\PiE$ means constitutive closure dependence on body/world-boundary variables. It does not mean biological embodiment or felt bodily ownership.
\item $\PiT$ means temporal horizon organization. 
\end{itemize}

\begin{remark}[Why the targets are not decorative]
The bridge targets earn their place only because each one induces a new comparator burden, a new intervention burden, a new artifact burden, and a new governance burden. If a proposed target induced none of these, it would be discarded as decorative vocabulary.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Predicate-to-burden map}
The six bridge predicates are best understood not as six new labels but as six new burden packages.

\paragraph{The burden of $\PiD$.}
To take phenomenal differentiation seriously, the program must show more than that the system has many internal states or rich semantic discriminability. It must show that differentiated organization is indexed, selective, and not exhausted by narration. This induces comparator burden against semantic-density and narration rivals, intervention burden against perspective detachment and semantic vividness controls, and artifact burden through a dedicated differentiation report and later reducibility outputs.

\paragraph{The burden of $\PiV$.}
To take valence structure seriously, the program must show that evaluative organization has internal topology: local asymmetries, organized gradients, or mode-structured deformation under perturbation. Scalar reward or self/non-self asymmetry alone is insufficient. This induces comparator burden against bookkeeping rivals, intervention burden through valence flattening and negative controls, and artifact burden through valence structure and intervention outputs.

\paragraph{The burden of $\PiW$.}
To take world richness seriously, the program must show that the system's self-indexed world organization is not merely competence or scene complexity. The system must exhibit bridge-relevant world organization that tracks indexed dependence, not just successful prediction. This induces comparator burden against richness-without-interiority rivals, intervention burden through world-richness compression, and artifact burden through world-richness reports and later candidate summaries.

\paragraph{The burden of $\PiA$.}
To take ambiguity bearing seriously, the program must show stable unresolved alternatives with downstream consequence. Static uncertainty scores or fast branching and pruning do not suffice. This induces comparator burden against ambiguity-resolution rivals, intervention burden through targeted collapse operations, and artifact burden through ambiguity reports with live-alternative fields.

\paragraph{The burden of $\PiE$.}
To take embodied closure seriously, the program must show constitutive dependence on body/world-boundary variables. Generic closed-loop control is not enough. This is the bridge predicate with the weakest current runtime grounding and therefore the one that most strongly demands explicit future instrumentation. Its artifact burden is correspondingly strict.

\paragraph{The burden of $\PiT$.}
To take temporal lived structure seriously, the program must show horizon organization that exceeds continuity and persistence. This requires temporal fragmentation tests, horizon-like dependencies, and explicit rival defeat against continuity-only models. In practical terms this burden is one of the main reasons why the bridge program cannot be replaced by Paper 8 plus rhetoric.

The global lesson is simple. Every bridge predicate is a tuple of obligations:
\[
\Pi_k \rightsquigarrow
(\text{estimator},\text{rivals},\text{interventions},\text{artifacts},\text{governance}).
\]
The bridge program succeeds only if all five components are eventually instantiated for each $k\in\{D,V,W,A,E,T\}$.

\section{Phenomenal Bridge Ontology}
\subsection{Bridge state}
The bridge state is introduced as
\[
\BridgeState_t=
\left\langle
\widehat{\Pi}_{D,t},
\widehat{\Pi}_{V,t},
\widehat{\Pi}_{W,t},
\widehat{\Pi}_{A,t},
\widehat{\Pi}_{E,t},
\widehat{\Pi}_{T,t},
{\BridgeMargin}_t,
\IntervProf^{\mathrm{b}}_t
\right\rangle,
\]
where ${\BridgeMargin}_t$ denotes the bridge-level irreducibility margin and $\IntervProf^{\mathrm{b}}_t$ denotes the bridge-level intervention profile. The bridge state does not replace the Paper 8 state. It is layered above it. The ontological relation is therefore
\[
\Substate_t \prec \BridgeState_t,
\]
not because the bridge state is metaphysically deeper, but because every bridge coordinate depends on the prior existence of a self-indexed subjectivity structure.

\subsection{Typing discipline}
The coordinates of $\BridgeState$ are heterogeneous in a way parallel to, but stronger than, the heterogeneity of the Paper 8 state.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item $\widehat{\Pi}_{D,t},\ldots,\widehat{\Pi}_{T,t}$ are bounded estimator-valued coordinates.
\item ${\BridgeMargin}_t$ is a model-comparison margin defined only when the comparator family exists.
\item $\IntervProf^{\mathrm{b}}_t$ is a structured intervention response object, not a scalar.
\end{itemize}
This heterogeneity matters because bridge support cannot be reduced to a single scalar early in the pipeline. The paper explicitly rejects any attempt to jump directly from provisional surfaces to a single bridge score while the comparator and intervention layers are missing.

\subsection{Coordinate dependence on the Paper 8 state}
Each bridge coordinate depends asymmetrically on the Paper 8 coordinates.

\paragraph{Phenomenal differentiation.}
$\widehat{\Pi}_{D,t}$ depends most strongly on $\SelfIndex_t$, $\OwnField_t$, $\Persp_t$, and $\Irred_t$. Differentiation here is not generic representational richness. It is differentiated organization that matters relative to a privileged self-locus.

\paragraph{Valence structure.}
$\widehat{\Pi}_{V,t}$ depends most strongly on $\ValAsym_t$, $\OwnField_t$, and $\IntervProf_t$. Paper 8 already closes valuation asymmetry; the bridge layer asks whether that asymmetry organizes into a richer topological structure than the upstream scalar burden requires.

\paragraph{World richness.}
$\widehat{\Pi}_{W,t}$ depends most strongly on $\Persp_t$, $\ContField_t$, and $\SelfIndex_t$. It asks whether the self-indexed locus sustains organized world-structure beyond mere perspective gain.

\paragraph{Ambiguity bearing.}
$\widehat{\Pi}_{A,t}$ depends most strongly on $\Persp_t$, $\ValAsym_t$, and the temporal organization implicitly carried by $\ContField_t$ and $\IntervProf_t$. A system that resolves ambiguity immediately or merely records uncertainty can fail this predicate even if it remains subjectivity-positive.

\paragraph{Embodied closure.}
$\widehat{\Pi}_{E,t}$ depends on $\OwnField_t$, $\ContField_t$, and $\IntervProf_t$ together with additional body/world-boundary observables that Paper 8 does not require. This is why provisional readout construction can supply only a preliminary proxy surface for embodied closure.

\paragraph{Temporal lived structure.}
$\widehat{\Pi}_{T,t}$ depends most strongly on $\ContField_t$, $\Persp_t$, and $\ValAsym_t$. Temporal lived structure is stronger than continuity because it concerns horizon organization, retention/protention asymmetry, and temporally extended significance.

\subsection{Operational realization status inside the ontology}
The ontology is structured into incremental realization requirements: provisional readout construction provides provisional estimator surfaces for the first six coordinates, recorded as bounded local run-level records, whereas ${\BridgeMargin}_t$ and $\IntervProf^{\mathrm{b}}_t$ remain open pending dedicated bridge comparator and intervention harnesses in intervention specification and comparator specification.

\subsection{Collapse risks and rival readings}
Every bridge coordinate must be introduced together with the corresponding collapse risk.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item $\widehat{\Pi}_D$ can collapse into semantic density without self-indexed interior differentiation.
\item $\widehat{\Pi}_V$ can collapse into valence bookkeeping or policy shaping.
\item $\widehat{\Pi}_W$ can collapse into world-model competence without indexed world-richness.
\item $\widehat{\Pi}_A$ can collapse into uncertainty bookkeeping or fast ambiguity resolution.
\item $\widehat{\Pi}_E$ can collapse into competent sensorimotor control without constitutive closure.
\item $\widehat{\Pi}_T$ can collapse into continuity, memory, or persistence without horizon structure.
\end{itemize}
The ontology is therefore inseparable from the rival family. A bridge coordinate that has no live rival is suspicious because it is probably defined too loosely. Conversely, a bridge coordinate that cannot in principle be separated from all live rivals is not yet scientifically admissible.

\subsection{Why bridge coordinates are not ontological shortcuts}
The bridge ontology must not be mistaken for a metaphysical inventory. This restriction follows directly from the formal structure: each coordinate is defined as a constrained relation among observables, upstream subjectivity coordinates, comparators, interventions, and structured records, so the coordinates belong first to the mathematical model, then to operational instrumentation, and only conditionally---if warranted---to semantic interpretation.

The separation matters in four directions.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item A bridge coordinate is not an ontological primitive. It is a structured explanatory target.
\item A bridge coordinate is not identical to its runtime estimator. The estimator is a measurement-facing map and can be weak, provisional, or underdetermined.
\item A bridge coordinate is not identical to the language used to describe it. The language may underfit or overfit the formal target.
\item A bridge coordinate is not identical to its strongest available interpretation. Different interpretation classes remain live until comparator and intervention burdens are settled.
\end{enumerate}

This distinction is especially important for $\PiE$ and $\PiT$. It is tempting to read embodied closure or temporal lived structure as if they already named well-understood ontological properties. Inside the present framework they do not. They name burden packages whose ontological interpretation remains constrained by future comparator and intervention results.

A mode window carries its complete Paper VIII record, including the inherited certificates, threshold decisions, matched intervention outcomes and held-out losses. A transport extension adds a finite graph of such windows and an independently recorded relation between witness instances for each edge. Forgetting those relations recovers the local records unchanged. For the following finite domain each window has two instances, labelled $0,1$, of the same local certificate. The copies carry all the data of that certificate. A local relabelling of instances does not change the underlying certificate.

\begin{definition}[Coherent Transport of Local Witnesses]\label{def:coherent-local-witnesses}
A transport extension is coherent if a choice of one certified witness instance $w_i$ at each mode $i$ satisfies every independently established edge relation. An edge datum $(w,w')$ is accepted only when both labels are identified from its paired observations under the declared error budget.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[A Cycle Separates Local Certification from Coherent Transport]\label{prop:bridge-transport-cycle}
For any complete Paper VIII certificate on an admissible window, the three-mode transport extensions below preserve it in every window. One admits coherent transport and the other does not. Thus, on the domain of such extensions of certified windows, coherent transport cuts out a proper subclass whenever that local certificate domain is nonempty. Coherent transport does not factor through the tuple of local Paper VIII records.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Copy the local record to modes $A,B,C$ and duplicate the witness instance in each window. On edge $e$ prepare both witnesses $w=0,1$ and record $(w,w\oplus k_e)$ in that order. Here $\oplus$ is addition modulo two. These observations recover the unique edge map $w\mapsto w\oplus k_e$. For coordinatewise errors strictly less than $1/2$, nearest-label decoding is exact. At radius $1/2$, a reported $1/2$ is compatible with either label and cannot certify the edge.

Use $(k_{AB},k_{BC},k_{CA})=(0,0,1)$ in the negative instance. The first two edges force $w_A=w_B=w_C$ and the last forces $w_A=1-w_C$. No choice satisfies the cycle. In the positive instance use $(0,1,1)$; both $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,1,0)$ are coherent choices. The local record is identical across the two instances, so a decision using it alone would give the same result on both. More generally the cycle is coherent exactly when the XOR of its three edge bits is zero. The edge experiment can be realized by three two-state modes with the specified deterministic transfers. It uses no inference of edge data from the local scores.
\end{proof}

This is an explicit positive and negative witness for the transport condition. The class-level statement is relative to a complete local certificate, so each inherited clause is preserved by copying its witness. The finite edge dynamics alone do not realize the upstream system. The proper-inclusion question for fully bridge-admissible systems still requires a joint realization of the remaining BPF predicates, comparator, intervention and governance obligations. Paper VIII local scores alone cannot supply that realization.

\begin{corollary}[The Two Transport Cycles on a Nonempty Rational Record]\label{cor:transport-rational-record}
The joint rational measurement window of Paper VIII \cite{paper8}, identified there by \texttt{\detokenize{prop:joi}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{nt-}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{rational-}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{window}}, gives a nonempty local record for both cycles of Proposition~\ref{prop:bridge-transport-cycle}. At this measurement level, the transport-certified class is a proper subclass of the three-mode extensions of that record.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Attach the same realized seven-coordinate record, intervention responses and reserved losses to both witness instances in each mode. Its gate is $1/3$ and its scalar is $48^{-1/7}$ in every copy. In the cycle $(0,1,1)$, the assignments $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,1,0)$ satisfy the measured edge relations. In the cycle $(0,0,1)$, the first two edge equalities and the last edge flip are inconsistent. Thus the two observed transport outcomes differ on identical, explicitly realized local data.
\end{proof}

The rational measurement record verifies the local scoring, selective-response and rival-comparison clauses of Paper VIII. The inherited Paper V and VII class certificates are still required for the full premise of Proposition~\ref{prop:bridge-transport-cycle}. The six BPF predicate realizations, bridge-specific rival defeat, selective intervention support, and the artifact, drift and governance conditions remain separate obligations for full bridge membership.

\begin{corollary}[Transport Cycles with a Complete Native Indexed Certificate]
\label{cor:native-transport-record}
The asymmetric native infinite-dimensional certificate of Paper VIII provides
a nonempty complete local domain for Proposition~\ref{prop:bridge-transport-cycle},
including its inherited Paper V and VII clauses. The cycles $(0,1,1)$ and
$(0,0,1)$ therefore give, respectively, coherent and incoherent extensions
of one fully realized local certificate. Coherent transport is a proper
additional condition on these extensions, not a consequence of their local
seven-coordinate records.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Copy the complete native plant, protocol, causal grammar, thresholds, global
product comparison and seven measurements to each mode, and use two labelled
instances of that same certificate at each vertex. The local vector is
\[
 (1,1/2,1/2,1/3,64/266529,1,179/464),
\]
with gate $64/266529>1/8192$ and scalar greater than $1/16$ in every copy.
No inherited clause is supplied by a Boolean flag alone. Independently record
the same two-label edge experiments of Proposition~\ref{prop:bridge-transport-cycle}
at error strictly below $1/2$. The two even-parity assignments exist for
$(0,1,1)$, whereas the odd-parity cycle $(0,0,1)$ has none. The labels index
witness instances, not extra values of the local gate; changing an edge
relation leaves each complete local certificate unchanged.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Remaining full bridge obligations.}
This closes the nonempty-local-domain premise for the transport example,
not the nonemptiness of the full $\BridgeClass$. A joint realization must
still satisfy the other BPF organizational predicates, its bridge-specific
rival closure and calibrated signed margin, selective interventions, cross-mode
identification, drift, artifacts and governance conditions. The native
Paper VIII margin applies to its declared two-port causal grammar, not to
that larger bridge comparison. Hence the proper subclass just proved concerns
coherent transport of complete local certificates; a strict inclusion theorem
for full bridge-admissible systems is not claimed. No experiential or human
equivalence conclusion follows.

\subsection{Relabeling and Transport of Certified Tower Records}

\begin{theorem}[Relabeling Invariance and Cycle Criterion for Binary Transport]\label{thm:bridge-binary-transport-gauge}
Let a finite graph of mode windows have two certified witness instances per vertex and an independently certified bijection on each edge. In local binary labels, write this bijection as $w\mapsto w\oplus k_e$. Under a local relabeling $w_v\mapsto w_v\oplus a_v$, the edge bit for $e=uv$ becomes $k_e\oplus a_u\oplus a_v$. The XOR of the edge bits around each cycle is invariant. A coherent assignment exists exactly when the XOR vanishes on every fundamental cycle of a spanning forest, and there are then $2^c$ coherent assignments for $c$ connected components. For two collections of certified edge maps on the same graph, equality of their fundamental-cycle parities is equivalent to their differing by a local relabeling.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Relabeling conjugates the old edge bijection by the source and destination label changes, yielding the stated formula. Edge orientation does not affect the bit because each binary bijection is its own inverse. The added vertex labels cancel in pairs around a cycle. A coherent assignment satisfies $w_v=w_u\oplus k_e$ at each edge, so its cycle sums vanish.

Conversely, fix a label at the root of each tree of a spanning forest. Transport from that root along the unique tree paths defines one label at every vertex. A remaining edge satisfies its equation exactly when its fundamental cycle has parity zero. Thus a choice of one root label per component produces all and only the coherent assignments. For the last assertion apply the same criterion to the edgewise XOR of the two collections. Its vertex solution is precisely the relabeling that takes one collection to the other.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Coherent and Obstructed Transport on Certified Tower Windows]\label{prop:bridge-certified-tower-transport}
There are two three-window extensions of the same realized local tower records with respective edge bits $(0,1,1)$ and $(0,0,1)$. The records include the six Paper V witnesses, the Paper VII life and four finite subject-descriptor certificates on their declared protocols, and the Paper VIII measurement and return comparisons with their stated information budgets. The paired tower edge responses certify both extensions under phase error at most $\eta=3^{-10}/4$ turns. The first extension has two coherent assignments, and the second has none under any local relabeling.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Use the writable common support and the joint certificate of Paper VII \cite{paper5,paper7}. In each mode window attach the same joint record, including its separate preparations and schedules, and the writable-tower lesion and reserved-return records of Paper VIII \cite{paper8}. In particular the recorded margin for the public return comparison is zero. Both witness instances carry this same local record. The edge experiment tracks an instance by its prepared or decoded memory sign and is conducted separately from the local scoring trials. The three windows can all use one fixed tower profile for the edge trials. The mode names index the windows.

Prepare two matched replicas with the alternating tape, phase zero and memories $m=\pm1/8$. Encode a positive memory sign by $w=0$ and a negative one by $w=1$. For an edge bit $k_e$, apply the command $q=(-1)^{k_e}$ at $u=0$, without a write or a role probe. The first bit of the alternating tape is zero, so this command leaves phase zero and updates memory to $q m/2$. A subsequent neutral command at $u=0$ gives the early phase $r_0=m_{\rm after}/8$ modulo one and halves the memory again while preserving its sign. Thus the short signed phase gives the destination label $w\oplus k_e$. Use the two replicas to record both rows of each edge map. The source of the first edge is identified by preparation and the subsequent source labels are the preceding decoded labels.

Carry the two replicas through the three consecutive edges with their neutral diagnostics and without resetting memory. The alternating tape and profile stay fixed. At the $\ell$th diagnostic the magnitude of the clean short signed phase is
\[
 A_\ell=\frac{1}{128\,4^{\ell-1}},\qquad \ell=1,2,3 .
\]
The two signed values are separated by $2A_\ell$, and $\eta<1/2048=A_3$. Hence every edge row can be read from the observed phase despite the allowed error. At the last edge, error $1/2048$ permits the same phase-zero reading from either sign, so that diagnostic alone cannot certify its destination at that radius. This collision also holds for the full readout by shifting all phase channels equally and retaining the common tape reading.

The independent edge records have even parity for $(0,1,1)$ and odd parity for $(0,0,1)$. Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge-binary-transport-gauge} gives the two decisions and their invariance under all eight local relabelings. The local trial records are identical in both extensions, proving non-factorization through the tuple of those records.
\end{proof}

The resulting strict inclusion concerns the extensions of the realized records. The Paper V and VII certificates retain their declared domains. The Paper VIII record includes both the restricted lesion comparison and the public return tie. Full Paper VIII class membership would require its positive margin against the relevant complete rival family, and full BPF membership would further require the independent bridge burdens.

\subsection{Intervention Tests for Three Bridge Burdens}

Use the writable tower of Paper V and the local record of Proposition~\ref{prop:bridge-certified-tower-transport}. Keep that record attached to every comparison. The following trials vary new preparations and interventions and record new phase responses. Their margins concern individual burdens of $\PiD,\allowbreak \PiA,\allowbreak \PiT$ under the stated rivals and budgets. The estimators in the bridge gate require their own calibration and joint certificate.

\begin{proposition}[Role-Selective Differentiation on the Writable Tower]\label{prop:bridge-tower-differentiation}
For the two prepared role profiles, let $C_{pr}$ be the clean early-phase response to a probe of role $r$ after a reference write minus the response after a lesion, with one common sham between write and probe. With the declared probe at the transported address, $C_{pr}=\delta\mathbf 1_{\{p=r\}}$ for $\delta=1/(16\cdot3^7)$ turns. The best additive fit $a_p+b_r$ has uniform error exactly $\delta/2$. At error at most $\delta/16$ per phase reading the measured matrix still has distance at least $3\delta/8$ from all additive fits.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For each role profile prepare the alternating tape and zero memory. Make a reference write or the opposite write at site $3$, and advance a common neutral sham. This moves the active or lesioned bit to site $4$. The probe of site $4$ with role $r$ returns phase $\delta$ exactly when its role matches and the transported bit matches the reference. Memory remains zero. Taking the phase difference of the two write arms gives the matrix in the statement.

The mixed difference $C_{00}-C_{01}-C_{10}+C_{11}=2\delta$ vanishes for every additive rival. An entrywise error of at most $e$ can change it by at most $4e$, giving $e\ge\delta/2$. The constant fit $\delta/2$ attains equality. Error in the measured paired contrast is at most $\delta/8$, so the triangle inequality gives the stated noisy lower bound. Two reference writes give zero contrast. A rival with the measured role-by-probe interaction can reproduce the clean matrix. This comparison identifies role-selective causal differentiation beyond the additive rival.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Late Choice Between Two Live Alternatives]\label{prop:bridge-tower-ambiguity}
Prepare two independent uniform activity bits at tape sites $3,4$ and advance one common sham. A uniform query drawn after the rival's memory is fixed selects one of the transported sites $4,5$. The matching-role probe identifies the selected bit with zero error at phase noise at most $\delta/16$. Under this state and query law the optimal error of a rival retaining one bit before the query, with no subsequent tape reading, is exactly $1/4$. A write during the advance can erase the first alternative while preserving the second.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
At one fixed role profile with zero memory, encode two binary choices by matching or opposing the alternating reference at sites $3,4$. Complement and shift transport each activity bit to the corresponding new site. Under the matching-role probe, active and inactive give phase $\delta$ and zero, separated by threshold $\delta/2$ under the declared noise. Both alternatives thus remain capable of changing the observed result until the query. A write opposing the reference at site $4$ during the advance forces the first transported bit inactive. The query at site $5$ still returns the independently prepared second bit. This is a selective collapse intervention on the available outcomes.

For each of its at most two memory values a deterministic rival has a pair of answers, one for each query. These at most two reconstruction words can exactly match at most two of the four equally likely prepared words. The sum of Hamming errors over the four words is at least two. Normalizing by the eight equally likely word-query pairs gives error at least $1/4$. Retain the first bit and use a fixed answer for the second to attain the bound. Randomized rules with randomness independent of preparation and query are mixtures of deterministic encoder-decoder pairs and satisfy the same lower bound. With two retained bits, the rival can also predict both responses exactly. The separation concerns maintenance of jointly available alternatives under the declared one-bit bottleneck.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Order-Dependent Return and an Exact Temporal Horizon]\label{prop:bridge-tower-temporal-order}
On a common preparation with first bit one, zero memory and phase zero, run command orders $(+1,0)$ and $(0,+1)$ with all phase inputs zero and no writes or probes. After one common $+1$ and $h$ further common $+1$ steps, the current public readings and command multisets coincide. The subsequent neutral early-phase gap is exactly $D_h=1/(384\cdot 2^h)$ turns. The two terminal reading sets are disjoint under phase error at most $\eta=3^{-10}/4$ precisely for integer $0\le h\le8$. An order-blind predictor with the same snapshot and multiset has mean clean absolute circular loss at least $D_h/2$ in the balanced pair, and an order-informed predictor attains zero.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Write the memory update as in Paper VI \cite{paper6}, with $a=1/2$, $\lambda=1/12$ and $\kappa=1/8$. The leading bit is zero after the first step in either order. The two-step memories are therefore $1/24$ and zero. A common positive command gives both arms phase zero and halves the memory difference. Each additional positive command repeats this effect. The tape and profile agree at every matched time. A final neutral command reads the memory difference with multiplier $\kappa$, giving $D_h$.

The two clean terminal phases are zero and $D_h$, with $D_8=1/98304>2\eta$ and $D_9=1/196608<2\eta$. Below the overlap radius the midpoint decision separates the signs of the centered contrast. When $D_h\le2\eta$, the midpoint can occur in either arm. The collision extends to all phase channels by common phase shifts, with the tape reading fixed. For prediction from the order-blind record the two outcomes must share a prediction. The circular triangle inequality bounds the sum of the two losses below by $D_h$, attained by any point on the intervening short arc. The order-informed predictor uses the stated transition and predicts each clean response exactly. Ablating memory before the terminal command makes the responses coincide. The resulting horizon measures temporal order information beyond a current-state readout and an unordered command history.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Open Evaluative, World and Body/World Burdens}

\begin{proposition}[Native Trace Equivalence and Extra Bridge Variables]\label{prop:bridge-native-trace-obstruction}
Let an extended model project to the same writable tower state by $\pi$. For every native control $u$ let its transition $\widetilde F_u$ and readout $\widetilde R$ satisfy $\pi\widetilde F_u=F_u\pi$ and $\widetilde R=R\pi$, with the same projected initial law and observation noise. Then all native observation and intervention transcripts have the same law, even for observation-adaptive native controls. A target that differs between extensions satisfying these conditions cannot be identified from those transcripts or from the inherited record computed from them.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Use the same projected initialization, noise and control randomization in the two experiments. At each step their matching observations give the same control and the commutation with $\pi$ gives the same next projected state. Induction gives identical transcripts under the coupling. Any statistic of the transcripts consequently has the same law in the two experiments. For a concrete pair, adjoin a fixed bit $z$ with native projection and readout as above and use $z=0$ versus $z=1$. A new test that adds $z/8$ turns to the neutral phase command separates the two completions while all native traces agree. This example concerns identification of an added coupling.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Valence structure.}
The return task has a scalar circular loss and a fixed phase reference. In the neutral alternating sector without a probe, reflecting phase, memory and phase command together preserves both circular return loss and absolute command cost. A structured evaluative topology for $\PiV$ needs an independently varied evaluation variable and a response that distinguishes its organization from the scalar recovery law.

\paragraph{World richness.}
The native comparison supplies commands from a recorded schedule. To test $\PiW$, an indexed world state must have an independently specified dynamics and perturbation, with an observable consequence at matched command complexity. Unobserved completions preserving native traces fall under Proposition~\ref{prop:bridge-native-trace-obstruction}.

\paragraph{Embodied closure.}
The neutral response and tape probes expose feedback within the tower. The $\PiE$ test requires distinct typed body and world variables, an intervention that breaks their coupling at matched control cost, and a measured consequence specific to that boundary. The native transcript alone leaves such completions observationally equivalent when their projections coincide.

The three rational experiments provide local witnesses for the indicated components. The conjunctive BPF claim still requires the open predicates, calibrated thresholds, joint intervention selectivity, the full bridge comparator margin, and the artifact, drift and governance obligations.

\section{A Minimal Coupled Body--World Extension}

The native trace obstruction separates identification from realization. The following extension retains the writable tower as an exact factor and gives new, separately typed ports for the previously unavailable interventions. All conclusions in this section concern the stated operational tasks and rival classes.

\begin{definition}[Projected two-port system]\label{def:bridge-two-port-system}
Let $x=(p,\theta,b,m)$ belong to the writable support of Papers V--VIII, with native transition $F_c$ and readout $R$. Adjoin body and world vectors $\mathbf b,\mathbf w\in[-1,1]^2$. Put
\[
 \alpha=\beta=h=\tfrac14,\quad \chi=\tfrac18,\quad
 J=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
 L_0=\tfrac12(I+J),\quad
 Q_+=\tfrac12\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
The indexed frame $P(p)$ is the swap matrix at role profile $p_1$ and the identity at every other catalogue profile. For a world projection $Q\in\{I,Q_+\}$, an evaluation map $L$ with $\|L\|_\infty\le1$, a motor gain $|\mu|\le1$, and an evaluation switch $\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}$, define
\begin{align*}
 x'&=F_c(x), &s&=P(p)Q\mathbf w, &f&=\chi L(s-\mathbf b),\\
 \mathbf b'&=\alpha\mathbf b+\beta s+\varepsilon f+a_{\rm b},\\
 \mathbf w'&=\alpha Q\mathbf w+\beta\mu QP(p)^{-1}\mathbf b+Qd.
\end{align*}
Here $a_{\rm b},d\in[-h,h]^2$ are distinct body and world input ports. Normal evaluation uses $L=L_0$. Native lifts use $Q=I$, $\mu=1$, $\varepsilon=0$ and zero port inputs. The native and additional readouts are respectively $R\pi$ and $(\mathbf b,\mathbf w)$, where $\pi(x,\mathbf b,\mathbf w)=x$.
\end{definition}

The time-unrolled causal graph has arrows $\mathbf b_t\to\mathbf b_{t+1},\mathbf w_{t+1}$ and $\mathbf w_t\to\mathbf b_{t+1},\mathbf w_{t+1}$, with the displayed input arrows and the prepared profile determining $P$. There is no port-state arrow to $x_{t+1}$. A motor intervention replaces only the coefficient of $\mathbf b_t\to\mathbf w_{t+1}$; its audit label is not an additional input to other mechanisms. This modularity is an explicit causal assumption. The evaluation force acts in the dynamics before the reserved measurements; it is not an output label.

\begin{theorem}[Exact Projection, Stability and Inherited Certificates]\label{thm:bridge-port-preservation}
Every lifted update satisfies $\pi\widetilde F_{c,a_{\rm b},d}=F_c\pi$. The section $\iota(x)=(x,0,0)$ is invariant under native lifts. On a common profile, use
\[
 \widetilde d=\max\{d_{\rm core},\|\Delta\mathbf b\|_\infty/4,
                                      \|\Delta\mathbf w\|_\infty/4\}.
\]
The native branch contraction remains at most $9/16$. The entire intervened port family contracts in its fibre by at most $3/4$ under common inputs and preserves $[-1,1]^4$. All previously certified native protocols, with their original readouts, comparison sectors and information budgets, have exactly the same records and margins in this extension.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
