\subsection{A Nonadditive Evaluative Branch and Its Comparator Boundary}

The shared-state obstruction above is retained without alteration: coordinate-linear forces add to the original normal operator. To separate a genuinely coordinate-additive evaluator, the target must have a nonzero mixed response. This changes the evaluative branch, not the projected tower or the number of dynamic states.

\begin{definition}[Coordinate-Additive Port Comparators]\label{def:bridge-additive-port-class}
On a fixed preparation and reference frame, a coordinate-additive evaluator is a map $G(v_1,v_2)=g_1(v_1)+g_2(v_2)$ with arbitrary functions $g_i:[-1,1]\to\mathbb R^2$. No continuity or linearity is required. Its channels have no access to a cross-coordinate message that makes either function depend on both coordinates. A fixed common initial condition is allowed. A state-dependent or sequential composition that communicates both coordinates need not belong to this class. The loss is the uniform maximum-norm error of the reserved world response, or its maximum on the four corners when stated explicitly.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Exact Rectangular Criterion for Additivity]\label{prop:bridge-rectangular-additivity}
For a map $F:A\times B\to V$ into a real vector space and nonempty $A,B$, coordinate additivity is equivalent to vanishing of every rectangular difference
\[
 F(x,y)-F(x,y')-F(x',y)+F(x',y')=0.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
An additive map cancels term by term. Conversely fix $(x_0,y_0)$ and put $g_1(x)=F(x,y_0)$ and $g_2(y)=F(x_0,y)-F(x_0,y_0)$. The rectangular identity with base point $(x_0,y_0)$ gives $F(x,y)=g_1(x)+g_2(y)$. Thus the condition is exact without regularity assumptions. A nonzero difference is a certificate against this class, rather than against every architecture described as modular.
\end{proof}

Let $u=(1,0)$, $\kappa=1/8$, and $\sigma\in\{0,1\}$. For an evaluation-input projection $R\in\{I,Q_+\}$ replace only the force of Definition~\ref{def:bridge-two-port-system} by
