\end{enumerate}
The first belongs to intervention specification and records intervention family performance, selectivity, controls, and collapse semantics. The second belongs to comparator specification and records bridge rival comparison, admissible hybrid comparison, loss decomposition, and bridge margin. Without these records, the higher-tier root verdicts cannot be justified.

\subsection{Campaign-level records}
At cross-experiment integration, the bridge framework further specifies:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item condition-level bridge summaries;
\item batch-level bridge summaries;
\item campaign-level bridge summaries;
\item a bounded candidate-facing bridge projection;
\item a bounded audit-facing bridge handoff summary;
\item a bridge verification matrix.
\end{itemize}
These campaign-level records remain formally specified and unpopulated at the baseline specification.

\subsection{Machine-readable specification}
The bridge record family is structured for deterministic verification even at the formal specification level. Every schema must declare at least:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item record role and authority class;
\item provenance and lineage constraints;
\item permitted and blocked interpretive claims;
\item required fields;
\item explicit non-implications.
\end{itemize}
Structured schemas form part of the formal burden architecture because they fix what empirical evidence would be required and which overreads remain blocked.

\subsection{Realization status across requirements}
Across realization requirements, the current baseline is explicitly bounded:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item formal specification specifies machine-readable protocol, axiom, rival, threshold, record-schema, interpretation-boundary, and surface-policy structures.
\item provisional readout construction defines six provisional run-level estimator surfaces as bounded local diagnostics.
\item No root bridge verdict records (calibrated gate evaluation) are populated.
\item No bridge intervention records (intervention specification) are populated.
\item No bridge reducibility records (comparator specification) are populated.
\item No campaign candidate or audit records (cross-experiment integration--B6) are populated.
\end{itemize}
This asymmetry reflects the deliberate boundary between formal specification and unexecuted empirical validation.

\section{Bridge Functional, Gate, and Taxonomy}
\subsection{Functional}
On admissible evidence with the six normalized predicate scores and the normalized intervention summary $\widehat{\Delta}_{\mathrm b}$ in $[0,1]$, use
\[
 \PhiBridge=\prod_{k\in\{D,V,W,A,E,T\}}\widehat{\Pi}_k^{\alpha_k}
 \cdot\widehat{\BridgeMargin}^{\alpha_R}
 \cdot\widehat{\Delta}_{\mathrm b}^{\alpha_\Delta},
\]
where all exponents are positive and fixed by the protocol. Use $0^\alpha=0$ for $\alpha>0$. If any required score is undefined, report insufficient evidence for the functional. For valid inputs its value lies in $[0,1]$ because every factor lies in $[0,1]$. Tie and defeat both give zero contribution through the normalized margin and retain their distinct raw values. The calibration scale and thresholds are task-specific and remain to be fitted and audited at calibrated gate evaluation.

\subsection{Gate}
For valid evidence the bridge gate is conjunctive. Missing required evidence leaves its evaluation insufficient:
\begin{align*}
 m_\Gamma
 &=\min\{\widehat{\Pi}_D,\widehat{\Pi}_V,\widehat{\Pi}_W,\widehat{\Pi}_A,
          \widehat{\Pi}_E,\widehat{\Pi}_T,\widehat{\BridgeMargin},\widehat{\Delta}_{\mathrm b}\},\\
 \GateBridge
 &=\mathbf1\bigl[m_\Gamma\ge\vartheta_\Gamma
 \land\BridgeMargin>\vartheta_R\land\mathsf{tr}\land\mathsf{cmp}\\
 &\hphantom{=\mathbf1\bigl[}\land\mathsf{int}\land\mathsf{drift}\land\mathsf{gov}\bigr].
\end{align*}
where $\vartheta_R\ge0$ is a raw-loss threshold, $\mathsf{tr}$ is verified coherence on the declared mode graph, and $\mathsf{cmp}$ denotes comparator validity, $\mathsf{int}$ denotes intervention stability, $\mathsf{drift}$ denotes absence of stale or drift invalidation, and $\mathsf{gov}$ denotes governance compliance. The threshold slots
\[
\Thresholds=
\{\vartheta_D,\vartheta_V,\vartheta_W,\vartheta_A,\vartheta_E,\vartheta_T,\vartheta_R,\vartheta_\Delta,\vartheta_{\Gamma}\}
\]
exist today only symbolically. They remain intentionally uncalibrated prior to calibrated gate evaluation.

\subsection{Non-compensation}
Bridge gate logic inherits the non-compensation principle from Paper 8. High strength on one coordinate may not compensate for collapse on another. High functional value may not rescue a failed gate. Positive provisional surfaces may not compensate for absent comparator or intervention layers. Non-compensation guards against treating scalar trade-offs as conjunctive satisfaction.

\begin{proposition}[Bridge functional does not imply bridge gate]
No value of $\PhiBridge$ by itself implies $\GateBridge=1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By construction the gate additionally depends on conjunctive threshold slots and non-scalar validity conditions for comparator support, intervention stability, drift cleanliness, and governance compliance. Since those conditions are not reducible to the value of the scalar functional, the implication fails.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Invalidation}
The bridge gate must invalidate on at least four families of failure:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{drift invalidation}: stale bridge records, stale upstream subjectivity records, stale threshold specifications, stale rival specifications, or stale governance rules;
\item \textbf{comparator invalidation}: bridge rival family changed or comparator run missing;
\item \textbf{intervention invalidation}: targeted intervention support absent or unstable;
\item \textbf{governance invalidation}: prohibited surfaces opened or claim-facing leakage detected.
\end{enumerate}
No later realization tier may downgrade these invalidation conditions to advisory warnings while asserting bridge admissibility.

\subsection{Taxonomy}
The bridge taxonomy should distinguish formalization state from support state. A disciplined taxonomy is:
\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.15}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}lY@{}}
\toprule
Status & Meaning \\
\midrule
\texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{formaliz}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ation_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{incomple}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{te}} & Formal bridge specification or target missing. \\
\texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{surface_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{only}} & Provisional bridge surfaces exist, but comparator, intervention, gate, or ecosystem burdens remain open. \\
\texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{evaluati}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{unsuppor}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ted}} & Root evaluation attempted without required comparator/intervention support. \\
\texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{gate_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{fail}} & Full bridge evaluation exists and conjunctive gate fails. \\
\texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{reducibl}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{e}} & Comparator evaluation exists and the signed margin is non-positive; this records no strict comparative advantage, not an automatically attained structural reduction. \\
\texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{interven}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{tionally_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{unstable}} & Intervention layer exists but fails selectivity or stability. \\
\texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{internal_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{admissib}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{le}} & Full bridge burden stack satisfied under framework-internal reading. \\
