The current baseline status is \texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{surface_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{only}}, which the present framework enforces strictly.

\section{Staged Operational Realization Pathway}
\subsection{Why staged realization belongs in the specification}
Staged operational mapping belongs in Paper 9 for the same reason it belonged in Paper 8: once a theoretical layer relies on structured observables and comparative verification, specifying its operational interface determines whether its formal objects can be instantiated, audited, falsified, and invalidated.

\subsection{Formal Specification}
formal specification defines the formal bridge commitments: protocol constraints, axiom and rival specifications, symbolic threshold parameters, schema contracts, interpretation boundaries, governance policies, non-claims, and structural consistency checks. It establishes formal specification discipline only.

\subsection{Provisional Coordinate Surfaces}
provisional readout construction defines provisional run-level estimator surfaces through six coordinate mapping modules paired with a provisional surface assembler, emitting six bounded run-level coordinate reports. Neither a calibrated bridge gate, nor a bridge comparator suite, nor a bridge intervention harness is realized at this tier.

\subsection{Bridge Intervention Family}
intervention specification specifies a bridge intervention harness comprising a formal intervention registry, perturbation executors, matched controls, and delta normalizers targeting the \texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{interven}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{tion_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{profile}} record. Until realized, the bridge remains interventionally incomplete.

\subsection{Bridge Comparator Family}
comparator specification specifies the executable comparator suite: a bridge rival executor, an admissible hybrid composer, a penalized bridge-loss evaluator, and a reducibility record emitter targeting \texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{reducibi}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{lity_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{report}}. Until realized, the bridge remains comparator-unsupported.

\subsection{Threshold Binding and Conjunctive Gate}
calibrated gate evaluation requires binding empirical calibration scales for the symbolic threshold slots, finalizing the functional $\PhiBridge$, evaluating the conjunctive gate $\GateBridge$, and authorizing root verdict schemas (\texttt{\detokenize{phenomen}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{al_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{projecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on}}, \texttt{\detokenize{phenomen}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{al_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{gate_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{result}}, and \texttt{\detokenize{phenomen}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{al_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{manifest}}). Only at this tier does bridge support language become operationally meaningful.

\subsection{Campaign and Ecosystem Integration}
cross-experiment integration requires bridge records to be integrated into condition-, batch-, campaign-, candidate-, and audit-facing governance with strict lineage binding, stale/drift invalidation, bounded summary outputs, and explicit claim-surface boundaries.

\subsection{Final Bridge Stack Audit}
final admissibility review requires comprehensive end-to-end verification and readiness classification, permitting internal admissibility classifications such as \texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{internal_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{admissib}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{le}} or \texttt{\detokenize{bridge_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{reducibl}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{e}} only once the intervention, comparison, calibration and integration requirements are verified intact.

