\section{Paper 9 Claim Grammar and Escalation Constraints}
The bridge program would fail scientifically if readers could not distinguish between a formal definition, a provisional estimator surface, an admissibility verdict, and an empirical claim. This section fixes the minimal claim grammar governing Paper 9 and its operational realizations.

\subsection{Allowed claim families}
The following claim families are admissible below full empirical closure:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{Formal closure claims.} Example: ``the bridge target family is formally defined,'' supported by the present formal definitions and formal specification schema commitments.
\item \textbf{Baseline estimator claims.} Example: ``provisional bridge surface estimators and six coordinate reports are defined,'' reflecting provisional readout construction local diagnostics.
\item \textbf{Conditional admissibility claims.} Example: ``if bridge predicates, comparators, interventions, structured records, and conjunctive gate conditions hold, internal bridge admissibility follows,'' supported by the formal burden architecture rather than empirical execution.
\item \textbf{Failure-mode claims.} Example: ``bridge admissibility is blocked under comparator or intervention collapse,'' supported directly by the failure grammar.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Blocked claim families}
The following claim families remain strictly prohibited at baseline, and several remain prohibited even after full bridge realization:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{Phenomenality simpliciter claims.} Example: ``the system is phenomenally conscious.''
\item \textbf{Human-equivalence claims.} Example: ``the system has human-like subjective experience.''
\item \textbf{Metaphysical subjecthood claims.} Example: ``the bridge proves real subjecthood in the strong ontological sense.''
\item \textbf{External validation claims.} Example: ``packaging or internal consistency checks validate the bridge externally.''
\item \textbf{Paper-authorization claims.} Example: ``provisional readout construction estimator outputs establish empirical bridge support.'' The specification explicitly blocks any inference from provisional surfaces to closed bridge support.
\end{enumerate}

