\subsection{Relabeling and Transport of Certified Tower Records}

\begin{theorem}[Relabeling Invariance and Cycle Criterion for Binary Transport]\label{thm:bridge-binary-transport-gauge}
Let a finite graph of mode windows have two certified witness instances per vertex and an independently certified bijection on each edge. In local binary labels, write this bijection as $w\mapsto w\oplus k_e$. Under a local relabeling $w_v\mapsto w_v\oplus a_v$, the edge bit for $e=uv$ becomes $k_e\oplus a_u\oplus a_v$. The XOR of the edge bits around each cycle is invariant. A coherent assignment exists exactly when the XOR vanishes on every fundamental cycle of a spanning forest, and there are then $2^c$ coherent assignments for $c$ connected components. For two collections of certified edge maps on the same graph, equality of their fundamental-cycle parities is equivalent to their differing by a local relabeling.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Relabeling conjugates the old edge bijection by the source and destination label changes, yielding the stated formula. Edge orientation does not affect the bit because each binary bijection is its own inverse. The added vertex labels cancel in pairs around a cycle. A coherent assignment satisfies $w_v=w_u\oplus k_e$ at each edge, so its cycle sums vanish.

Conversely, fix a label at the root of each tree of a spanning forest. Transport from that root along the unique tree paths defines one label at every vertex. A remaining edge satisfies its equation exactly when its fundamental cycle has parity zero. Thus a choice of one root label per component produces all and only the coherent assignments. For the last assertion apply the same criterion to the edgewise XOR of the two collections. Its vertex solution is precisely the relabeling that takes one collection to the other.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Coherent and Obstructed Transport on Certified Tower Windows]\label{prop:bridge-certified-tower-transport}
