Use the writable common support and the joint certificate of Paper VII \cite{paper5,paper7}. In each mode window attach the same joint record, including its separate preparations and schedules, and the writable-tower lesion and reserved-return records of Paper VIII \cite{paper8}. In particular the recorded margin for the public return comparison is zero. Both witness instances carry this same local record. The edge experiment tracks an instance by its prepared or decoded memory sign and is conducted separately from the local scoring trials. The three windows can all use one fixed tower profile for the edge trials. The mode names index the windows.

Prepare two matched replicas with the alternating tape, phase zero and memories $m=\pm1/8$. Encode a positive memory sign by $w=0$ and a negative one by $w=1$. For an edge bit $k_e$, apply the command $q=(-1)^{k_e}$ at $u=0$, without a write or a role probe. The first bit of the alternating tape is zero, so this command leaves phase zero and updates memory to $q m/2$. A subsequent neutral command at $u=0$ gives the early phase $r_0=m_{\rm after}/8$ modulo one and halves the memory again while preserving its sign. Thus the short signed phase gives the destination label $w\oplus k_e$. Use the two replicas to record both rows of each edge map. The source of the first edge is identified by preparation and the subsequent source labels are the preceding decoded labels.

Carry the two replicas through the three consecutive edges with their neutral diagnostics and without resetting memory. The alternating tape and profile stay fixed. At the $\ell$th diagnostic the magnitude of the clean short signed phase is
\[
 A_\ell=\frac{1}{128\,4^{\ell-1}},\qquad \ell=1,2,3 .
\]
The two signed values are separated by $2A_\ell$, and $\eta<1/2048=A_3$. Hence every edge row can be read from the observed phase despite the allowed error. At the last edge, error $1/2048$ permits the same phase-zero reading from either sign, so that diagnostic alone cannot certify its destination at that radius. This collision also holds for the full readout by shifting all phase channels equally and retaining the common tape reading.

The independent edge records have even parity for $(0,1,1)$ and odd parity for $(0,0,1)$. Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge-binary-transport-gauge} gives the two decisions and their invariance under all eight local relabelings. The local trial records are identical in both extensions, proving non-factorization through the tuple of those records.
