The first component of the update proves the projection identity for every port input, not only at zero input. Induction, with coupled initial core states, observation noise and controller randomization, gives equality of every native transcript under observation-adaptive native controls. Zero fibre is invariant under the native lifts, so $\iota$ is an isometric, dynamically invariant copy of the original plant.

The body difference under a common mechanism has norm at most
\[
 (\alpha+\varepsilon\chi)\|\Delta\mathbf b\|_\infty
 +(\beta+\varepsilon\chi)\|\Delta\mathbf w\|_\infty,
\]
and the world difference is bounded by $\beta\|\Delta\mathbf b\|_\infty+\alpha\|\Delta\mathbf w\|_\infty$. Thus the fibre bound is $3/4$. The input bounds give body norm at most $3/4+h=1$ and world norm at most $1/2+h=3/4$. With native lifts the fibre bound is $\alpha+\beta=1/2$, so the original $9/16$ core bound still dominates. The safe support is compact; the zero-input fibre attracts to zero. The native switched attractor is the original attractor times zero fibre.

Transport the existing preparations by $\iota$ and keep the same native instruments and rival sectors. The full profile acquisition and catalogue gap $1/80$, split-sector integration interval $[1/216,1/24]$, continuity and differentiation witnesses, prefix windows, phase repair and subject-descriptor comparisons are unchanged. The five lesion measurements and the public return tie of Paper VIII are unchanged as well. These are the published protocol-relative certificates; adding port readings does not silently enlarge their comparison sectors or supply a positive upstream margin that was absent. Port pulses in the new trials start from zero fibre, have norm at most $h$, and subsequent unforced updates contract, so they stay within the compact fibre kernel of radius $1/2$ inside the safe cube.
