All input vectors have the same two typed slots and bound; no rival receives fewer command opportunities.

\begin{theorem}[Four-State Minimality of the Port Realization]\label{thm:bridge-port-minimality}
For either role and any motor gain, the block Hankel matrix
\[
 H_p=\begin{pmatrix}M_0&M_1\\M_1&M_2\end{pmatrix}
\]
has rank four, determinant $t^4$ and $\|H_p^{-1}\|_\infty=24$. Four fibre coordinates are sufficient and necessary for a linear realization of these ports. They are also necessary for any exact finite-dimensional realization with $C^1$ transitions and body readouts, in which world inputs leave the projected core trajectory fixed.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The two scalar blocks in each indexed coordinate are $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&t\\t&2\alpha t\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Their determinants are $-t^2$ and their inverses are $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}-2\alpha/t&1/t\\1/t&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. A signed permutation of coordinates does not change the maximum row-sum norm. Substitution gives the asserted determinant and inverse norm. Any linear realization of dimension $d$ factors its Hankel matrix as an observability matrix times a controllability matrix, hence has rank at most $d$.

For the differentiable statement, allow two independent small world inputs $u,v\in\mathbb R^2$ in the first two steps, followed by zero input. The two subsequent body readings are
\[
 (y_2,y_3)=(\beta P u,\,2\alpha\beta P u+\beta P v).
\]
Their Jacobian with respect to $(u,v)$ has determinant $\beta^4\ne0$. At time two every dependence on these inputs must pass through the fibre state: the core is fixed by projection and the future inputs are zero. Factoring the two readings through a $d$-dimensional $C^1$ state makes their Jacobian have rank at most $d$, so $d\ge4$. The displayed system attains four. Discontinuous exact encodings or access to retained external histories are different realization classes and are not excluded by this argument.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[A Quantified Indexed World Witness]\label{prop:bridge-indexed-world}
Let the reduced-world rival family contain every relay predictor whose port Hankel rank is at most two, and every predictor of arbitrary order whose relay response is independent of the prepared role. The available input alphabet, role record and command lengths are matched. A rank-two rival may allocate four registers, but any additional registers are unobservable or unreachable for this port transfer. Against this union, the normal model has uniform body-response error gap at least $1/96$, or $1/6$ in units of $t$. A world compression $Q=Q_+$ retains the entire common-mode relay response while reducing the Hankel rank to two and erasing the role contrast.
