\]
If every entry differs by at most $e$, the left side is at most $4e$, giving $e\ge1/96$. This bound covers all rank-at-most-two matrices and therefore all reduced-world realizations, including nonlinear encoders of parameters inside that linear transfer class. A role-independent fit has one matrix for the two targets $tI,tS$, whose entrywise distance is $t$. Its error is at least $t/2$, which is the larger bound. The two comparisons require both dynamical order and indexed transport, rather than scene size alone.

For $Q_+$ all port matrices have the corresponding common-mode projection. Since $P Q_+=Q_+$, their Hankel has rank two and is role-independent. If the input is $h(1,1)$, the world, body and every relay response remain in the common mode and are exactly the uncompressed responses. Thus the compression loses indexed structure without eliminating that control competence. A fully informed four-state predictor realizes the original transfer exactly. An arbitrary history-based emulator can do the same; the result does not exclude a rival merely because it calls its own state ``model-free.''
