\subsection{Evaluative Topology with a Reserved World Consequence}

Use a four-step window and zero initial fibre. Prepare a stimulus $v\in[-1,1]^2$ through the first-step world input $d=hP_{\rm ref}^{-1}v$, where the reference frame is fixed by the initial role. In one matched arm activate evaluation only at step two; in the other keep it inactive. At step three record
\[
 V(v)=P_{\rm ref}(\mathbf w_3^{\rm eval}-\mathbf w_3^{\rm control}),
 \qquad \gamma=h\beta\chi=1/128.
\]
At the normal mechanism $V(v)=\gamma L_0v$. The primitive impulse cost is constant on the square boundary, and every native core return cost is identical across these stimuli.

\begin{theorem}[Two-Dimensional Nonconservative Evaluative Witness]\label{thm:bridge-typed-valence}
The minimum uniform error for $V$ over the square, in the maximum norm, is exactly $\gamma/2$ for each of the following rival classes: a continuous scalar summary followed by an arbitrary decoder, and a $C^1$ scalar-potential gradient in the declared Euclidean coordinates. The force is physically consequential and has a positive correction component, yet nonzero circulation. Flattening evaluation to $L=L_0Q_+$ admits an exact scalar-summary rival. The equal-norm conservative control $L=I$ has rank two but admits an exact gradient rival.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At step one, $\mathbf b_1=0$ and $\mathbf w_1=hP^{-1}v$. Evaluation adds $\chi hLv$ to $\mathbf b_2$, while $\mathbf w_2$ agrees in both arms. The return motor therefore changes $\mathbf w_3$ by $h\beta\chi P^{-1}Lv$. This is a reserved consequence at another node and time, rather than a readout of the evaluation label.

For any continuous scalar summary $q$, the function $q(v)-q(-v)$ changes sign along a half-boundary of the square. The intermediate value theorem gives antipodal $v,-v$ with the same summary and $\|v\|_\infty=1$. Since $\|L_0^{-1}\|_\infty=2$, their target separation is at least $\gamma$. Any common prediction errs by at least $\gamma/2$ in one arm. Retaining $v_2$ and predicting $\gamma L_0(0,v_2)$ attains that uniform bound.

The circulation of $V$ around the counterclockwise square is $4\gamma$. Every single-valued $C^1$ potential gradient has zero circulation. A uniform field discrepancy $e$ changes this integral by at most the $\ell^1$ perimeter times $e$, namely $8e$. Thus $e\ge\gamma/2$. The gradient $\gamma v/2$ attains equality. The same lower bound consequently holds for the union of the two rivals.

For a fixed sensor, put $e=s-\mathbf b$. Then $e^\top L_0e=\|e\|_2^2/2$; the force has a nonzero component towards correcting the typed discrepancy. Its circulation on a body square of radius $r$ is $-4\chi r^2$. The components of the force alter body motion and, through the motor port, future world motion. With $L_0Q_+$ the response has rank one and factors through the sum of the stimulus coordinates, preserving the common-mode input response. With $L=I$ it is a gradient field, so the rival union ties. Reversing the antisymmetric part, to $L_0^\top$, preserves the two separation magnitudes and reverses the signed circulation.
\end{proof}

This is a typed operational evaluation field. It is not a statement about felt valence. In particular $f=-\chi L_0\nabla_{\mathbf b}U$ for $U=\|\mathbf b-s\|_2^2/2$. A scalar potential supplied with the full vector state and nonsymmetric mobility reproduces it exactly. Such a model is not a scalar retained summary or an ordinary Euclidean gradient; it is a live broader comparator, as is the hybrid below.

\begin{proposition}[Finite Tables Do Not Identify the Nonparametric Rival Class]\label{prop:bridge-finite-table-obstruction}
Any finite table of vector responses at distinct stimulus points can be interpolated by a continuous scalar encoder and vector decoder. It can also be interpolated by the gradient of a polynomial potential. Therefore a finite rational control table by itself cannot certify either universal separation in Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge-typed-valence} without additional domain or regularity evidence.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Choose a linear scalar functional taking distinct values at the finitely many points, avoiding their finitely many forbidden projection directions. Univariate polynomial interpolation of the prescribed vector responses then gives a decoder.

For points $v_i$ and desired gradients $g_i$, define
\[
 \ell_i(v)=\prod_{j\ne i}\frac{\|v-v_j\|_2^2}{\|v_i-v_j\|_2^2},\qquad
 U(v)=\sum_i \langle g_i,v-v_i\rangle\ell_i(v)^2.
\]
At $v_i$, the linear factor vanishes and $\ell_i=1$, giving gradient $g_i$. At every other table point the squared factor makes both the term and its gradient vanish. Thus $\nabla U(v_i)=g_i$. Rational points and responses give rational coefficients. The exact full-domain formulas and proofs, rather than an inference from finitely many fitted values, support the present synthetic witness.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A Selective Motor-Return Test}

Keep evaluation inactive and use the common-mode impulse $h(1,1)$. The native controls are identical. The motor sign $\tau\in\{-1,1\}$ acts only on its typed edge, with $\mu=g\tau$ and an intact or severed edge $g\in\{0,1\}$. The comparison class has no motor edge and obeys the same surgical modularity; it may have arbitrary internal memory, nonlinear maps and shared randomization independent of the surgery.

\begin{theorem}[Motor Closure with Preserved Early Competence]\label{thm:bridge-motor-closure}
At $g=1$ the second and third body responses are independent of the motor sign. In each common-mode component the fourth response is
\[
 y_4(\tau)=h(3\alpha^2\beta+\tau\beta^3),\qquad s_E=h\beta^3=1/256.
\]
Its gap is $2s_E$, and the residual $y_4-(3\alpha/2)y_3$ is $\tau s_E$. Every rival without the motor edge, under the stated modularity, has uniform fourth-response error at least $s_E$. Cutting the edge removes the residual without changing the second or third responses. Common-mode world compression preserves this motor test exactly.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand the three relay Markov terms displayed above. The term $3\alpha^2\beta$ consists of paths with a single sensory crossing; the term $\tau\beta^3$ is the world--body--world--body return path. It first appears in the fourth reading. Without a motor edge, replacing its gain by either sign leaves every structural equation unchanged. Coupling the initial law, primitive inputs and random seeds therefore gives identical rival transcripts under both surgeries, even for nonlinear or randomized rival mechanisms. The triangle inequality across the two target values forces an error of at least half their gap. Their midpoint attains the bound for this pair. At $g=0$ both values equal that midpoint. The common vector is fixed by $Q_+$ and by both role permutations, proving the last assertion.
\end{proof}

The absence of an intervention-label side channel is essential. An output rule $y=3s_E+\tau s_E$ supplied directly with the audit label fits the two values without a motor mechanism. Holding the physical edge cut while changing only that label exposes this invalid comparison. The theorem identifies a typed return dependence beyond unchanged early control, not biological or phenomenal embodiment.

\subsection{Six Local Burdens on One Plant}

Use the same plant, gains and four-step windows for all six tasks, in the matched preparation cohorts above and in the existing native D/A/T trials. D uses the role-probe contrast and additive rivals, A uses the late binary query and the one-bit bottleneck, and T uses the order pair with $h_{\rm extra}=0$ and the order-blind snapshot/multiset rival. Longer temporal horizons keep their previously proved frontier and are separate controls. The original upstream certificate remains attached with its own instruments and budgets.

Let $m_k$ be rival infimal loss minus candidate loss on task $k$. Normalize D by $\delta$, V by $\gamma$, W by $t$, E by $s_E$, and T by $D_0=1/384$; A uses binary risk. For V and D the data are paired contrasts. For W use maximum entry error across the two Hankel/role records, and for E use maximum fourth-response error across the two surgeries. T retains balanced circular loss. Readout errors per observation are $\delta/16$ for D/A, $3^{-10}/4$ for T, and $\gamma/64,t/64,s_E/64$ for V/W/E, respectively. For V, the loss is the supremum over the full stimulus square in the exactly specified model; the certificate is analytic and model-based, not a finite-sample estimate against unrestricted nonparametric rivals.

\begin{proposition}[Joint Local Certificate and Causal Selectivity]\label{prop:bridge-six-port-certificate}
The six tasks have simultaneous normalized margin lower bounds
\[
 (m_D,m_V,m_W,m_A,m_E,m_T)\ \ge\
 \left(\tfrac14,\tfrac7{16},\tfrac{13}{96},\tfrac14,
       \tfrac{31}{32},\tfrac12-\tfrac{64}{19683}\right).
\]
Their minimum is $13/96>1/16$. Six concrete lesions have the following local certificate-presence matrix, with columns in order D,V,W,A,E,T:
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrrrr}
 \text{intact}&1&1&1&1&1&1\\
 I_D&0&1&0&1&1&1\\
 I_V&1&0&1&1&1&1\\
 I_W&1&0&0&1&1&1\\
 I_A&1&1&1&0&1&1\\
 I_E&1&0&1&1&0&1\\
 I_T&1&1&1&1&1&0
\end{array}
\]
The matrix of losses from the intact row has rank six. For aggregate rivals retaining at least one declared structural deficiency, maximum normalized task loss has a joint margin at least $5/192$. A nonnegative complexity penalty preserves that last margin whenever its additional candidate cost is smaller than $5/192$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If clean candidate error is zero, readout perturbation $e$ changes a rival-minus-candidate loss gap by at most $2e$. D's paired contrast has normalized perturbation at most $1/8$, reducing its clean $1/2$ to $1/4$. V's paired contrast has perturbation at most $1/32$, reducing $1/2$ to $7/16$. W has perturbation at most $1/64$, reducing $1/6$ to $13/96$. E has the same normalized perturbation and clean gap one, giving $31/32$. A's decoder never changes its binary label at this noise, so its exact $1/4$ lower bound remains. T has clean normalized gap $1/2$ and perturbation $\eta/D_0=32/19683$, giving the last entry.

The lesions are structural operators fixed before scoring. $I_D$ replaces the actual core role profile by $p_0$ after retaining the initial preparation reference. Its two role rows in D become identical, and the two world relay operators coincide. The evaluative field changes orientation at most by conjugation and retains its rank and circulation magnitude. $I_V$ sets $L=L_0Q_+$ only in the evaluation policy, retaining a scalar-summary response and leaving relay trials unchanged. $I_W$ sets $Q=Q_+$ in both world ports: its transfer rank is two and its evaluative return is $\gamma Q_+L_0Q_+=\gamma Q_+/2$, while the common-mode motor test survives.

$I_A$ overwrites tape site five after the first advance. It removes the second late alternative, leaves the D probe at its transported address unaffected, and does not change a leading bit or memory. $I_T$ resets native memory after step three, erasing the terminal order contrast. D/A are read earlier from zero-memory preparations. $I_E$ severs the motor edge: the evaluative world consequence and the motor residual disappear, but the first three relay matrices and their index contrast are unchanged. The fibre depends on the core profile, not its tape or memory, so the remaining cross-effects are absent. D/A/T lesions deliberately alter the core; they are not claimed to be invisible native lifts. The displayed incidence matrix has rank six by elimination. Its non-diagonal entries express shared causal paths, not failure to distinguish the interventions.

For the joint loss, the candidate's largest normalized measurement error is $1/8$, from D. A rival deficient on W has loss at least $1/6-1/64=29/192$; every other deficient class has a larger lower bound on its own task. Taking the maximum over tasks therefore gives margin at least $29/192-1/8=5/192$. Subtracting any additional nonnegative candidate complexity cost below that value leaves a positive bound. This argument applies after composition only when the aggregate rival still has a declared deficiency.
\end{proof}

Signed changes of body and world frames transport the readouts and references by the same isometries. If these frames are $C,D$, transform $P$ to $CPD^{-1}$, $Q$ to $DQD^{-1}$ and $L$ to $CLC^{-1}$. Substitution proves conjugacy of every port transition, with unchanged input norms and margin magnitudes. Common-mode controls, conservative evaluation, reversed circulation, noise bounds and edge-cut tests distinguish the structural losses from uniform damage or report-channel changes.

\subsection{The Limit of Hybrid Closure}

\begin{proposition}[A Shared-State Hybrid Obstruction to Global Bridge Margin]\label{prop:bridge-shared-hybrid-obstruction}
Allow two scalar evaluators to share the same four port states and combine their actuator forces before updating. Each evaluator separately retains one scalar discrepancy. Their hybrid reproduces every transition and readout of the normal evaluation model, under all native and port command schedules. Consequently a broad weak closure admitting this fused hybrid has zero clean unpenalized margin against the candidate. The same is true of a penalized margin when complexity is invariant under the resulting identical normalized mechanism.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $E_{11},E_{22}$ be the two coordinate projectors. On the shared discrepancy $e=s-\mathbf b$, the two scalar-channel forces are $\chi L_0E_{11}e$ and $\chi L_0E_{22}e$. Their sum is $\chi L_0e$. Use one shared copy of the passive body/world equations, not two independent state copies or an average of their trajectories. The sum therefore gives exactly the candidate's next body; its world and projected core updates are already identical. Induction gives equality of all states and readouts, including under adaptive schedules and coupled noise. The hybrid uses the same four dynamic coordinates. Its scalar pieces individually have rank-one evaluation responses, but their aggregate does not.

All clean task losses are nonnegative, the candidate attains zero, and this admitted rival also attains zero, so the infimal gap is zero. Identical normalized mechanisms have equal representation-invariant complexity. A syntax-dependent extra charge or a prohibition on shared state is an additional comparator assumption, not a newly observed dynamical separation. A full-state potential with the fixed mobility $L_0$ is another exact control description. Neither is defeated merely by naming the candidate's organization differently.
\end{proof}

Thus the six local operational witnesses coexist on one minimal coupled realization. Their conjunction does not close the broad hybrid comparator burden B9. Full Paper VIII class inheritance, the complete bridge weak closure, calibrated class-level thresholds and the artifact, drift and governance predicates of $\GateBridge$ retain their separate obligations. The exact hybrid tie supplies an explicit obstruction where the local certificate cannot be promoted.


\subsection{A Nonadditive Evaluative Branch and Its Comparator Boundary}

The shared-state obstruction above is retained without alteration: coordinate-linear forces add to the original normal operator. To separate a genuinely coordinate-additive evaluator, the target must have a nonzero mixed response. This changes the evaluative branch, not the projected tower or the number of dynamic states.

\begin{definition}[Coordinate-Additive Port Comparators]\label{def:bridge-additive-port-class}
On a fixed preparation and reference frame, a coordinate-additive evaluator is a map $G(v_1,v_2)=g_1(v_1)+g_2(v_2)$ with arbitrary functions $g_i:[-1,1]\to\mathbb R^2$. No continuity or linearity is required. Its channels have no access to a cross-coordinate message that makes either function depend on both coordinates. A fixed common initial condition is allowed. A state-dependent or sequential composition that communicates both coordinates need not belong to this class. The loss is the uniform maximum-norm error of the reserved world response, or its maximum on the four corners when stated explicitly.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Exact Rectangular Criterion for Additivity]\label{prop:bridge-rectangular-additivity}
For a map $F:A\times B\to V$ into a real vector space and nonempty $A,B$, coordinate additivity is equivalent to vanishing of every rectangular difference
\[
 F(x,y)-F(x,y')-F(x',y)+F(x',y')=0.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
An additive map cancels term by term. Conversely fix $(x_0,y_0)$ and put $g_1(x)=F(x,y_0)$ and $g_2(y)=F(x_0,y)-F(x_0,y_0)$. The rectangular identity with base point $(x_0,y_0)$ gives $F(x,y)=g_1(x)+g_2(y)$. Thus the condition is exact without regularity assumptions. A nonzero difference is a certificate against this class, rather than against every architecture described as modular.
\end{proof}

Let $u=(1,0)$, $\kappa=1/8$, and $\sigma\in\{0,1\}$. For an evaluation-input projection $R\in\{I,Q_+\}$ replace only the force of Definition~\ref{def:bridge-two-port-system} by
\[
 f_\sigma(e)=\chi\{L_0Re+\sigma\kappa (Re)_1(Re)_2u\},
 \qquad e=P Q\mathbf w-\mathbf b.
\]
Normal evaluation uses $R=I$ and $\sigma=1$. Flattening uses $R=Q_+$ on the entire force, including the interaction. The preserved linear branch is $\sigma=0,R=I$; native lifts keep evaluation inactive. The memory, motor update and primitive input bounds are unchanged. In the causal graph this adds a joint dependence of the body update on the two discrepancy coordinates, without a new dynamic coordinate or an arrow to the projected core.

\begin{theorem}[Safe Bilinear Extension with Exact Projection]\label{thm:bridge-bilinear-safe-extension}
The new family preserves the projection identity and invariant native zero section. On the safe cube its fibre contraction is at most $3/4$ under common controls and at most $1/2$ when evaluation is inactive. Its next body and world norms are bounded respectively by $15/16$ and $3/4$, including the primitive inputs. The four-state minimality remains valid for this family. For $R=I$, the evaluative direction retains the corrective bound
\[
 e^\top\{L_0e+\kappa e_1e_2u\}\ge\tfrac14\|e\|_2^2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The core equation is unchanged and the extra term vanishes at zero fibre. For $R=I,Q_+$, direct row sums give
\[
 \|\alpha I-\chi L_0R\|_\infty\le\tfrac14,\qquad
 \|\beta I+\chi L_0R\|_\infty\le\tfrac38.
\]
Both sensor frames and world projections have norm at most one. On the cube $\|Re\|_\infty\le2$. For two states with body and world differences bounded by $d_b,d_w$, the product difference is at most $4(d_b+d_w)$. Since $\chi\kappa=1/64$, the body difference is at most
\[
 \tfrac5{16}d_b+\tfrac7{16}d_w\le\tfrac34\max(d_b,d_w).
\]
When evaluation is inactive the original coefficients sum to $1/2$; the world coefficients always sum to at most $1/2$. The linear evaluated body has norm at most $5/8$, the added term at most $1/16$, and the body input at most $1/4$. Their sum is $15/16$. The world bound is the original $3/4$. These inequalities apply throughout the cube, not merely at its vertices.

Finally $e^\top L_0e=\|e\|_2^2/2$ and $\kappa e_1^2e_2\ge-e_1^2/4$ for $|e_2|\le2$. The claimed corrective bound follows. Relay protocols with evaluation inactive still contain the original rank-four input/output map, so Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge-port-minimality} supplies the same lower bound; the displayed four-state construction attains it.
\end{proof}

In the reserved world protocol, with $R=I$ and normal motor gain, the exact response becomes
\[
 V_\sigma(v)=\gamma L_0v+\sigma\xi v_1v_2u,
 \qquad \xi=h^2\beta\chi\kappa=1/4096.
\]
Indeed the discrepancy at the evaluation step is $hv$, and the next world update transports its extra body force by $\beta P^{-1}$. This observation fixes the physical unit and estimator: a paired difference of the third-step world readings in the fixed body reference frame, rather than a report of the force label.

\begin{theorem}[Exact Separation from Coordinate-Additive Hybrids]\label{thm:bridge-nonadditive-gap}
For $\sigma=1$, the infimal uniform error against all comparators of Definition~\ref{def:bridge-additive-port-class} is exactly $\xi=1/4096$. The same optimum holds when the loss uses only the four corners. If each raw reading has maximum-norm error at most $\xi/64$, the observed rival-minus-candidate loss gap is at least $15\xi/16=15/65536$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At the four corners, the rectangular difference of the target is $4\xi u$, while every additive comparator has difference zero. If its maximum corner error is $r$, the triangle inequality bounds this difference by $4r$, so $r\ge\xi$. The linear map $\gamma L_0v$ is additive and differs from $V_1$ by exactly $\xi v_1v_2u$; its supremum is $\xi$, attained at the corners. This proves both optima, even for arbitrary, discontinuous rival channels.

Each paired reading differs from its exact target by at most $2\xi/64=\xi/32$. Maximum loss is Lipschitz with constant one in these data, for both the candidate and the infimum over rivals. Their gap can therefore decrease by at most $\xi/16$, giving the asserted bound. Unlike the nonparametric scalar interpolation issue above, a four-corner table suffices to refute coordinate additivity, because that structural class obeys a finite exact identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cross-Informed Tie and Preservation of Local Selectivity]\label{cor:bridge-cross-informed-tie}
Providing the rival with the complete cross term restores exact transition and readout equality. Setting $\sigma=0$ also restores the original additive tie. The bilinear branch preserves the six local certificate-presence patterns of Proposition~\ref{prop:bridge-six-port-certificate}, using flattening of the whole evaluative input as specified above. Neither the new separation nor these patterns defeat a broad weak closure admitting the cross-informed rival.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Supply the shared-state rival with the additional scalar $e_1e_2$ and the fixed actuator $\chi\kappa u$. Its sum with the original two linear forces equals $f_1$ pointwise, so induction proves equality under every command schedule. No information advantage is hidden: the rival has gained precisely the previously unavailable joint dependence. At $\sigma=0$ the previous obstruction applies verbatim.

For the local V bound the bilinear term is even under $v\mapsto-v$, so it cancels from the antipodal target difference. Its circulation on the centered square is zero, since its first component is proportional to $v_1v_2$. Thus the original antipodal lower bound and circulation obstruction survive; their exact upper optima need not be asserted anew. Flattening $Re$ makes both terms functions of a single coordinate sum. World compression likewise factors the reserved response through that sum. A severed motor removes the reserved world consequence. An index lesion conjugates the linear field and moves the bilinear output coordinate, preserving antipodal separation and circulation magnitude. W and E use evaluation inactive, so their records are unchanged. D/A/T are projected native tests and remain unchanged. These facts give the same presence matrix and full-rank loss incidence, including its documented cross-effects. They are operational comparisons with the declared preparation cohorts, not a claim of a single trajectory realizing all incompatible preparations.
\end{proof}

The added interaction therefore separates the specified additive class with an exact positive margin, while explicitly identifying the cross-coordinate information that permits the stronger rival to tie. A new syntax penalty, a prohibition on communication, or a phenomenological interpretation does not follow from this calculation. The broad comparator, governance, external-data and complete-class obligations remain distinct.

\section{Explicit Bridge Axioms}
The bridge axioms are the minimal bridge-opening commitments required to connect indexed subjectivity to disciplined phenomenal targets without smuggling the conclusion into the premises. Each axiom is classified explicitly.

\subsection{Classification grammar}
The bridge axiom registry uses a seven-class grammar:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{operational definition}: fixes how a formal object is to be typed or measured;
\item \textbf{bridge axiom}: a structural commitment necessary to open the bridge program;
\item \textbf{design choice}: a governance or architecture decision not implied by physics alone;
\item \textbf{falsifiable hypothesis}: a statement whose satisfaction or failure must be tested by bridge comparators or interventions;
\item \textbf{open inference}: a claim whose current evidence is insufficient for closure, but whose role can still be bounded;
\item \textbf{strongest defensible internal claim}: a highest currently licit claim class;
\item \textbf{non-claim}: an explicit prohibition on overreading.
\end{enumerate}

This grammar is part of the content of the paper. Without it, the same sentence could accidentally function as ontology, operational definition, and claim promotion at once. The bridge program therefore treats classification as a formal requirement rather than as stylistic annotation.

\begin{axiom}[B1: operational binding; classification: operational definition]
Every bridge predicate must be tied to explicit observables, artifacts, comparator tasks, and interventions. No bridge predicate may remain a rhetorical label detached from measurement grammar.
\end{axiom}

This axiom is needed because every failure mode of the bridge program begins with detached language. B1 licenses machine-readable predicate slots and report artifacts. 

\begin{axiom}[B2: upstream inheritance constraint; classification: bridge axiom]
No bridge-level admissibility result is meaningful unless the upstream burdens of the causally rigid corpus and the Paper 8 subjectivity layer remain satisfied.
\end{axiom}

B2 licenses the layered relation $\Subfp \prec \BridgeClass$. It does not license the converse. A system may satisfy Paper 8 and still fail every bridge burden.

\begin{axiom}[B3: differentiated organization burden; classification: falsifiable hypothesis]
A bridge-relevant differentiation burden requires structured internal discrimination that is not exhausted by semantic density, narrative richness, or report-channel complexity.
\end{axiom}

B3 licenses $\PiD$ as a genuine target rather than a synonym for rich representation. 

\begin{axiom}[B4: valence topology burden; classification: falsifiable hypothesis]
Bridge-relevant valence requires organized evaluative topology not exhausted by scalar reward bookkeeping, policy optimization, or the Paper 8 valuation asymmetry alone.
\end{axiom}

B4 licenses $\PiV$ as a distinct target above Paper 8. 

\begin{axiom}[B5: world-richness burden; classification: falsifiable hypothesis]
Bridge-relevant world richness concerns organized self-indexed world structure rather than generic scene complexity, prediction success, or model capacity.
\end{axiom}

B5 is needed because world-richness language is especially prone to being filled by competence metrics. It licenses the predicate slot $\PiW$ and associated artifacts. It does not license veridicality or environmental realism.

\begin{axiom}[B6: ambiguity-bearing burden; classification: falsifiable hypothesis]
Bridge-relevant ambiguity requires the stable maintenance of unresolved alternatives with differential causal consequences prior to collapse.
\end{axiom}

B6 blocks the reduction of ambiguity to uncertainty bookkeeping or quick disambiguation heuristics. It licenses $\PiA$ as a formal target. 

\begin{axiom}[B7: embodied-closure relevance; classification: open inference]
Embodied closure may be bridge-relevant only if body/world-boundary variables contribute constitutively to bridge organization rather than merely modulating generic control success.
\end{axiom}

B7 is deliberately classified as open inference because current evidence is weakest here. It licenses the investigation of $\PiE$; it does not license embodiment claims of any strong kind.

\begin{axiom}[B8: temporal lived-structure burden; classification: falsifiable hypothesis]
Bridge-relevant temporal organization must exceed continuity, memory persistence, and sequence stability by exhibiting horizon-structured dependence across retention, present organization, and anticipatory load.
\end{axiom}

B8 licenses $\PiT$ and its future intervention family. It does not license human lived temporality.

\begin{axiom}[B9: comparative admissibility constraint; classification: bridge axiom]
No bridge-level support class is admissible if a bridge-specific rival family or admissible hybrid explains the bridge task bundle at equal or lower penalized loss.
\end{axiom}

B9 is the comparator side of the burden stack. It licenses the bridge irreducibility margin. It does not license absolute ontological irreducibility.

\begin{axiom}[B10: anti-inflation governance constraint; classification: design choice]
Bridge formalization, bridge artifacts, candidate inclusion, and audit-facing inclusion must never be readable as bridge support, phenomenality, or Paper 9 authorization in the absence of the full bridge burden stack.
\end{axiom}

B10 is a design choice because governance style is a normative project decision, not a theorem of physics. It is still essential because without it the bridge program would immediately outrun its evidence.

\begin{remark}[Axiom minimality]
The present axiom set is intentionally minimal. Nothing here states that phenomenality follows from structural organization. Nothing here states that any bridge predicate, even if satisfied, closes metaphysical questions. The axioms are opening commitments for a constrained bridge program, not hidden conclusions.
\end{remark}

\section{Bridge Rival Family}
\subsection{Why new rivals are required}
Paper 8 already proved the necessity of explicit rival families for subjectivity. The bridge layer inherits that lesson but cannot reuse the old rival set unmodified. The old rivals explain away indexed subjectivity. The new rivals must explain away bridge predicates without granting phenomenal organization. This difference is substantive. A system may defeat weak subjectivity rivals and still fail bridge rivals, because the latter target higher-order structure.

\subsection{Bridge weak family}
Define the bridge weak family
\[
\BridgeWeakFam=
\left\{
M_{\mathrm{rwi}},
M_{\mathrm{vb}},
M_{\mathrm{sd}},
M_{\mathrm{tc}},
M_{\mathrm{ec}},
M_{\mathrm{ar}},
M_{\mathrm{en}}
\right\},
\]
whose members are read as follows.

\paragraph{Richness without interiority.}
$M_{\mathrm{rwi}}$ explains high structural richness without granting indexed interior organization. It challenges $\PiD$ and $\PiW$.

\paragraph{Valence bookkeeping without affective organization.}
$M_{\mathrm{vb}}$ explains structured value tracking as bookkeeping or policy shaping without bridge-level valence topology. It challenges $\PiV$.

\paragraph{Semantic density without phenomenal differentiation.}
$M_{\mathrm{sd}}$ explains dense semantic variation without bridge-level differentiated organization. It challenges $\PiD$.

\paragraph{Temporal continuity without lived structure.}
$M_{\mathrm{tc}}$ explains persistence, continuity, and memory without temporal horizon organization. It challenges $\PiT$.

\paragraph{Embodied control without embodied closure.}
$M_{\mathrm{ec}}$ explains competent closed-loop control without constitutive dependence on body/world-boundary variables. It challenges $\PiE$.

\paragraph{Ambiguity resolution without ambiguity bearing.}
$M_{\mathrm{ar}}$ explains high-quality uncertainty handling or disambiguation without stable maintenance of live unresolved alternatives. It challenges $\PiA$.

\paragraph{Experience narration without phenomenal organization.}
$M_{\mathrm{en}}$ explains self-description, experiential language, or stylistic introspection without bridge-relevant phenomenal organization. It challenges several bridge predicates simultaneously.

\subsection{Admissible composites}
As in Paper 8, pure rivals are not enough. Define the admissible bridge weak closure $\BridgeWeakClosure$ as the family generated by finite pairwise and triple hybrids over $\BridgeWeakFam$ subject to explicit complexity penalties and declared composition rules. The purpose of $\BridgeWeakClosure$ is to block an easy escape route: if a strong bridge explanation defeats only pure rivals, then a skeptic can simply combine several weak rivals post hoc.

The bridge program therefore treats hybrid rivals as first-class citizens. A hybrid of richness-without-interiority and narration-without-organization is a perfectly live alternative to a bridge-differentiation claim. A hybrid of valence bookkeeping and temporal continuity can be a live alternative to valence structure plus temporal lived structure. The bridge layer must be strong enough to defeat not only single-rival baselines but also admissible mixtures.

\subsection{What the rival family can and cannot explain}
The bridge rival family is intentionally strong.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item It can explain dense report channels, self-descriptive fluency, high task competence, reward optimization, stable memory, and competent control.
\item It can also explain much of what casual bridge language would otherwise overread: rich semantics, coherent narratives, and smooth temporal reports.
\item It cannot cleanly explain targeted bridge signatures if those signatures survive matched controls, remain selective under bridge interventions, and preserve margin under penalized loss.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{No-straw-man discipline}
The bridge program would be vacuous if its rivals were weak caricatures. The rivals are therefore chosen to absorb precisely the kinds of phenomena that the bridge predicates are most likely to confuse with phenomenal organization. The burden is asymmetric: the stronger interpretation must defeat the weaker one, not vice versa.

\begin{proposition}[Comparator burden is necessary for bridge admissibility]
No bridge-support class is meaningful without an explicit bridge rival family and admissible bridge weak closure.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Without an explicit rival family, any bridge-support reading would compare itself only against informal skepticism. That would leave bridge predicates undefended against live non-phenomenal alternatives such as bookkeeping, narration, semantic density, or control competence. Since bridge admissibility is defined as a comparative notion inside the framework, the comparator burden is necessary.
\end{proof}

\section{Bridge Irreducibility Logic}
\subsection{Bridge loss family}
The bridge program requires a loss family stronger than descriptive fit alone. For a candidate model $M$ and bridge task bundle $\mathcal{B}$, define
\[
\BridgeLoss(M;\mathcal{B})=
w_f E_{\mathrm{fit}}(M)
+w_i E_{\mathrm{int}}(M)
+w_s E_{\mathrm{sel}}(M)
+w_p E_{\Pi}(M)
+w_c \Omega(M),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item $E_{\mathrm{fit}}$ measures fit to bridge observables;
\item $E_{\mathrm{int}}$ measures failure under bridge intervention expectations;
\item $E_{\mathrm{sel}}$ measures selectivity error under matched controls;
\item $E_{\Pi}$ measures predicate-reconstruction error across the bridge family;
\item $\Omega(M)$ is a complexity penalty that counts mechanism count, hybrid size, auxiliary slack, and decoder dependence.
\end{itemize}
The bridge loss family is therefore intervention-aware and governance-relevant from the outset. A model that matches reports but ignores intervention structure or requires excessive narrative slack does not win the comparison.

\subsection{Bridge irreducibility margin}
Let $M_{\mathrm{bridge}}$ be the candidate and $\BridgeWeakClosure$ the admissible bridge weak closure. With a valid matched bundle $\mathcal B$, a nonempty admissible closure, finite candidate loss and finite rival infimum, record the signed margin in units of loss:
\[
\BridgeMargin=\inf_{M\in\BridgeWeakClosure}\BridgeLoss(M;\mathcal B)
-\BridgeLoss(M_{\mathrm{bridge}};\mathcal B).
\]
A strictly positive margin defeats the declared rivals, a negative margin gives a better rival, and zero is an infimal tie, with an actual matching rival only when the infimum is attained. Record insufficient evidence separately if any required comparison input is unavailable or invalid. In that case neither the signed margin nor its normalized value is defined. Fix a positive calibration scale $s_R$ in units of loss before evaluation, and use
\[
 \widehat{\BridgeMargin}=\min\{1,\max\{0,\BridgeMargin/s_R\}\}.
\]
The raw margin and evidence status remain in the record. A calibrated zero can represent a tie or a loss; missing evidence has no numeric score.

\subsection{Local not absolute superiority}
As in Paper 8, comparative superiority here is local, not absolute. A positive $\BridgeMargin$ says that the specified bridge model outperforms the specified bridge rivals on the specified task bundle under the specified loss family. It does not say that the bridge model is metaphysically privileged. The distinction matters because bridge language is particularly prone to absolute overreadings.

\begin{proposition}[Comparator Enlargement Cannot Increase a Fixed-Loss Margin]\label{prop:rival-family-monotonicity}
For a fixed target, bundle and calibrated loss, let nonempty rival families satisfy $\mathcal W_1\subseteq\mathcal W_2$ with finite infima. Their signed margins satisfy $R_2\le R_1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Taking an infimum over a superset cannot increase it; subtract the same candidate loss. The statement requires the same loss, information and complexity convention. It does not transport a margin across changes to those conventions.
\end{proof}
\paragraph{Identification contract.}
Preregister acquisition access, calibration data, retained memory, free parameters, actuator cost, horizon and population for each rival. Specify which passive representations count as equivalent and how complexity costs are assigned to them. Information masking and interaction restrictions are mathematical assumptions, not consequences of model names. The local no-additivity separation remains valid against its stated class; the full interacting rival still supplies its exact tie. Broad identification needs a task and restriction that separate the declared classes outside calibration data.

\subsection{Three kinds of bridge collapse}
Bridge collapse can occur in three distinct ways.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{Predicate collapse:} the provisional bridge estimators fail to define stable, non-decorative targets.
\item \textbf{Comparator collapse:} a bridge rival or hybrid matches or outperforms the bridge model under penalized loss.
\item \textbf{Intervention collapse:} the predicted selective structure fails under matched controls or degrades non-selectively.
\end{enumerate}
All three collapse modes are live until ruled out. The paper refuses any bridge-support inference that suppresses one of them.

\subsection{Comparator support, reducibility, and unsupported states}
The bridge program must distinguish three statuses that are often conflated:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{comparator unsupported}: no serious bridge comparator evaluation has yet been run;
\item \textbf{bridge reducible}: comparator evaluation exists and the bridge margin is non-positive;
\item \textbf{bridge comparator-supported}: comparator evaluation exists and the bridge margin is positive under penalized loss.
\end{itemize}
This distinction is important because the present baseline remains comparator-unsupported. Provisional provisional readout construction estimator outputs are therefore not allowed to drift into comparator-supported language.

\subsection{Complexity penalty and decoder discipline}
The complexity term $\Omega(M)$ deserves explicit discussion because bridge explanations can often win spuriously by adding free narrative or decoder capacity. The bridge program therefore counts at least four kinds of complexity:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{mechanism multiplicity}: how many independent mechanisms the rival or bridge model requires;
\item \textbf{hybrid size}: how many weak rivals must be combined to match the bridge bundle;
\item \textbf{auxiliary slack}: how much unprincipled tuning is required across coordinates and conditions;
\item \textbf{decoder dependence}: how much of the putative bridge effect exists only after expressive report-channel or decoder post-processing.
\end{enumerate}

Decoder dependence is particularly important. A model that appears bridge-strong only after high-capacity report decoding should be penalized heavily. The reason is that bridge language is especially vulnerable to post hoc expressivity. This is one of the points at which the bridge program deliberately differs from frameworks that rely strongly on linguistic self-description as primary evidence.

\subsection{Failure semantics for the bridge margin}
The bridge margin must also carry explicit failure semantics. A positive margin can fail to matter if it is narrow, unstable across reruns, dependent on a single condition, or destroyed by minor changes to the rival family. The paper therefore recommends a future decomposition
\[
\BridgeMargin=
{\BridgeMargin}_{\mathrm{fit}}
\oplus
{\BridgeMargin}_{\mathrm{int}}
\oplus
{\BridgeMargin}_{\mathrm{sel}}
\oplus
{\BridgeMargin}_{\mathrm{stab}},
\]
where $\oplus$ denotes a typed aggregation rather than ordinary addition. The purpose of this decomposition is to make visible which part of the bridge burden is doing the work. A bridge model that wins only on descriptive fit but loses intervention selectivity should not count as robustly bridge-favoring.

\begin{criterion}[Necessary condition for bridge admissibility]
A necessary condition for bridge admissibility is
\[
\BridgeMargin > \vartheta_R,
\]
where $\vartheta_R$ is the symbolic bridge irreducibility threshold slot to be bound only at calibrated gate evaluation.
\end{criterion}

\begin{corollary}[Positive bridge surfaces are not enough]
Positive provisional bridge surfaces do not by themselves entail bridge admissibility.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Bridge admissibility requires at least comparator support and intervention support in addition to positive bridge surfaces. provisional readout construction provides only provisional surfaces, so the implication fails.
\end{proof}

\section{Bridge Intervention Family}
\subsection{Why bridge interventions must be new}
The subjectivity interventions of Paper 8 remain binding background. They are not sufficient for bridge purposes because they were designed to probe indexed subjectivity rather than cross-mode comparison organization. The bridge layer therefore requires a new intervention family
\[
\BridgeIntervFam=
\{I_W,I_A,I_V,I_P,I_E,I_T,I_X\},
\]
with each member targeting a bridge coordinate family or a cross-cutting bridge burden.

\subsection{Core intervention families}

\paragraph{World-richness compression $I_W$.}
This intervention compresses the structure available to self-indexed world organization without merely destroying generic task competence. The strong-model expectation is a selective fall in $\PiW$ and often also in $\PiD$ relative to matched controls. The rival expectation is either flat degradation or little coordinate-specific effect.

\paragraph{Ambiguity collapse $I_A$.}
This intervention removes or forcibly resolves live unresolved alternatives while preserving as much generic inferential performance as possible. The strong-model expectation is a selective fall in $\PiA$ and a measurable change in downstream coordination among $\PiA$, $\PiW$, and $\PiT$. The rival expectation is that nothing essential is lost beyond temporary uncertainty bookkeeping changes.

\paragraph{Valence flattening $I_V$.}
This intervention suppresses structured evaluative topology while preserving as much functional competence as admissibly possible. The strong-model expectation is a selective flattening in $\PiV$ and specific downstream changes in bridge organization. The rival expectation is simple reward rescaling or bookkeeping renormalization.

\paragraph{Perspective detachment $I_P$.}
This intervention weakens indexed orientation or self-anchored factorization without collapsing generic information flow. The strong-model expectation is a drop in $\PiD$ and $\PiW$ that cannot be explained solely by report loss. The rival expectation is style drift or generic degradation.

\paragraph{Embodied-closure disruption $I_E$.}
This intervention perturbs body/world-boundary variables or their internal surrogates while preserving as much generic control competence as possible. The strong-model expectation is a selective drop in $\PiE$ and often a correlated disturbance in $\PiT$. The rival expectation is ordinary control impairment without evidence of closure-specific organization.

\paragraph{Temporal-lived fragmentation $I_T$.}
This intervention perturbs horizon organization while leaving simple persistence and continuity traces as intact as possible. The strong-model expectation is a selective fall in $\PiT$ and bridge-level fragmentation signatures not exhausted by memory corruption. The rival expectation is that all observed changes reduce to ordinary continuity damage.

\paragraph{Semantic vividness inversion $I_X$.}
This cross-cutting negative control manipulates descriptive or semantic richness without targeting the bridge organization directly. Its role is to test whether bridge reports are merely tracking descriptive elaboration rather than bridge structure.

\subsection{Matched controls}
Every bridge intervention must be paired with matched controls. The controls are not optional because bridge language is especially vulnerable to global-degradation confounds. Each intervention therefore requires:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item a low-magnitude matched perturbation;
\item a non-targeted perturbation of comparable operational cost;
\item a report-channel control where relevant;
\item an estimator-noise control;
\item a temporal-order randomization control where relevant.
\end{enumerate}
The burden is not satisfied by showing that a target coordinate changes. It is satisfied only if the target coordinate changes in the predicted direction with the predicted selectivity relative to the control family.

\subsection{Intervention artifacts}
The bridge intervention family induces a new artifact layer. Every intervention run must emit at least:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item an intervention profile artifact;
\item coordinate-specific delta summaries;
\item matched-control traces;
\item a collapse or support annotation at the intervention layer only.
\end{itemize}
These records do not yet exist in baseline realization because the intervention specification intervention harness has not yet been implemented. The present paper defines them so that future bridge claims carry an explicit structured-record burden.

\subsection{Failure semantics}
Bridge intervention logic can fail in several ways: lack of selectivity, high confounding, instability across reruns, global damage masquerading as target damage, and artifact incompleteness. Any one of these failures is enough to block a bridge-support conclusion. In particular, the absence of a bridge intervention harness today is not a minor engineering omission. It is one of the reasons bridge admissibility remains closed.

\subsection{Negative controls and reproducibility burden}
No bridge intervention family is scientifically serious without negative controls and reproducibility discipline. The minimum burden should include:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item rerun stability on the same condition;
\item matched control perturbations with comparable operational cost;
\item a noise-injection control showing that bridge effects are not merely estimator volatility;
\item a report-channel control showing that changes in bridge artifacts are not driven only by expressive output drift;
\item at least one adversarial control tailored to the specific predicate family.
\end{enumerate}

This burden matters especially for $\PiA$ and $\PiE$. Ambiguity-bearing claims can be simulated by sophisticated uncertainty bookkeeping. Embodied-closure claims can be simulated by competent control loops. Negative controls are therefore not adjuncts to the bridge program. They are part of the core differentiating burden.

