\section{Conclusion}
Paper 9 specifies the bridge program and proves the declared local realization, transport and interaction results. The bilinear extension separates coordinate-additive rivals, while a full interacting rival retains the exact tie. These results do not close the general bridge class. That definition is non-trivial. Before this paper, the corpus could say a great deal about the six-predicate operational criterion, viability-and-recovery, subjecthood, and indexed subjectivity, but it could not yet say, with equal rigor, what additional machinery would be required before phenomenal-bridge predicates become scientifically admissible targets. After this paper, that machinery is explicit.

The result is central to the corpus for structural reasons. The paper gathers the upstream theorem burdens, the Paper 8 subjectivity framework, the operational evaluation pathway, and the ordered bridge realization requirements into one integrated architecture. It orders what belongs upstream, what belongs at the bridge level, what belongs to operational realization, what belongs to governance, and what remains explicitly open.

What has been achieved is a change in explanatory grammar. ``Phenomenal bridge'' is no longer permitted to function as a loose aspiration. It now names a bridge-organization predicate family, an ontology, an axiom stack, a rival family, an intervention family, a structured record family, a gate architecture, and a governance discipline. This is as much formal specification as can be responsibly claimed without comparator closure, intervention closure, bridge gate execution, and multi-campaign hardening.

What the paper makes newly possible is equally clear. It makes the intervention and remaining realization requirements sharply specifiable. It makes bridge-support criteria evaluable rather than speculative. It makes future theorems about bridge irreducibility, bridge transport limits, and bridge admissibility meaningful. It also makes future failure meaningful: if the bridge program fails, it will fail against explicit burdens rather than against vague expectations.

What the paper does not settle is also clear. It does not close external validation. It does not convert any present system into a bridge-admissible system merely because formal specification and provisional readout construction are specified. Those remain the next burdens.

The research program therefore changes level once again. After Paper 8, the key problem was whether indexed subjectivity could be formalized without inflation. After Paper 9, the key problem becomes whether a fully burdened bridge program can survive rival defeat, intervention logic, artifact discipline, and governance hardening strongly enough to justify internal bridge admissibility. That is the right next problem. It is sharper, harder, and more falsifiable than the one that preceded it.

